const items0 = [["HG01", () => "This guide has an app coming soon! Working hard!\n\nHow to use this very large guide\n\nBegin with Quick start, learn one induction and the complete return, then use Let the next response guide you for the part no recording can decide in advance. The daily and weekly sections help you make the practice your own. The appendices hold extra tools for questions, NLP language, anxiety, daydreaming, altered states, hostile-presence experiences, sleep, influence, and additional inductions. Sources are together in Appendix K.", "30fcda86b0c3dde4b19cf4a8e3ef9d516ddf36b36e49d23e9a67b34471733f53"], ["HG02+03", () => "The table of contents is hiding on the left\n\nSubstack kinda hides the table of contents until you move the mouse toward the left side of the page. On a guide this long, that little hidden menu is your friend.\n\nIntroduction\n\nHello Lovey Bunny!\n\nYou were born with a mind that can dream, protect, analyze, imitate, love, remember, hide, reveal, and heal. Nobody taught its different layers how to speak to each other.\n\nInstead, most of us learned to push through, suppress emotion, perform confidence, obey or rebel against authority, and call that adulthood. Meanwhile, the deeper mind kept running its own programs: the child wanting safety, the guard expecting danger, the body remembering what the intellect had explained away, the intuition speaking before language, and the fantasy trying to become guidance.\n\nA lot of hypnosis teaching treats the subconscious like a machine waiting for commands. That never did much for me. I get more from sitting down and listening until the scared kid, the guard, the body, the intuition, and the part trying to run Tuesday can all be heard without one of them seizing the microphone.\n\nIn this guide, self-hypnosis gathers attention until the conscious mind, subconscious, body, inner child, protectors, adult self, and Higher Self can participate in one honest inner meeting.\n\nThe method is simple: listen, warm, protect, clarify, reality-test, and act.\n\nA dramatic trance is optional. The goal is a more trustworthy relationship with the whole mind, proven in ordinary life.\n\nCommunion is the closest word I have for that. The conscious mind stops shouting from the roof, and the subconscious no longer has to communicate only through symptoms, dreams, cravings, daydreams, and peculiar little body signals.\n\nI treat the subconscious as a living ecology of parts, memories, impulses, images, protections, and wisdom. I bring in inner-child reparenting because a wounded young part won\u2019t be repaired by a clever suggestion. I begin with Higher Self or the universal heart when the system needs coherence, and separate into parts only when that helps. I borrow from prompt engineering because the shape of a question changes the kind of answer we can hear. And I include influence literacy because hypnosis is hardly the only thing hypnotizing us.", "2fa0c5537cd743aa65ec411bf32371ca13ebcd4e6fededc670026315e61bada5"], ["HG04", () => "Quick start: do this first\n\nGather attention, notice what is here, offer one fitting response, wait, then come all the way back. You can meet a difficulty or develop love, curiosity, play, or confidence. Nothing dramatic has to happen.\n\nBefore the first session, read the induction and full return below while awake. Set aside time somewhere physically secure. Choose one purpose and what you are leaving alone today. Then put the screen aside; the practice is something you learn to conduct, not a checklist to keep reading.\n\nAs you listen, a sensation, image, phrase, feeling, objection, or silence may become noticeable. Let the next invitation follow that response. Use warmth, a boundary, direction, or more time as needed. Finish with the complete return, then note what happened and any real-world action it calls for.\n\nWhen you become panicky, disoriented, increasingly unreal, or less able to function, stop deepening and return to the room and appropriate support. A difficult feeling you freely choose to stay with while present is a different situation.\n\nDo you usually think in words?\n\nBefore choosing a script, notice how words actually work in your mind. Do silent words or phrases arise often, occasionally, rarely, or hardly at all? They needn\u2019t sound like an audible voice. Does this vary when you plan, remember, reflect, or feel upset? Can you deliberately form a silent sentence, even if words seldom arrive on their own?\n\nThat is different from feeling something before words come in a particular moment. Neither question requires deciding that feelings always come first. \u201cNot sure\u201d is a useful answer; this is a way to choose workable instructions, not a diagnosis.\n\nRead \u201csay,\u201d \u201cask,\u201d and \u201clisten\u201d in this guide as invitations, not a requirement for an inner narrator. Speak aloud, write, use a recording, or follow the invitation without rehearsing a sentence. The child and protector needn\u2019t speak in sentences either. Don\u2019t assume an image must appear instead. Try whatever is accessible, and change the route when it stops helping.", "accb52be18bdcf5d88df91c9c074dbe6177de25664a00a6f8c978bde6a7b6f50"], ["HG05", () => "What I mean by hypnosis\n\nAPA Division 30 commonly defines hypnosis as focused attention, reduced peripheral awareness, and increased responsiveness to suggestion. That\u2019s basically true, but there\u2019s a lot more than meets the eye. In fact, hypnosis is such a broad and common type of altered state that it\u2019s hard to confine it to one use. But I\u2019m using hypnosis here as a therapy.\n\nAnd hypnosis as a therapy is as old as time itself. While the modern technique of it was discovered by Franz Anton Mesmer in the 1770s, shamans have been using dancing, music, and drugs to induce hypnosis for therapeutic purposes forever. We enter into trance states every night which resemble hypnosis as we go to sleep and as we wake up as well. Those states can also be used for deep therapy.", "47eef79adf9bf346363427da94f7b3826054c7f8c12353fa5eead4e005800725"], ["HG06", () => "Protecting Yourself from Hypnotists\n\nHypnotism can happen in a therapist\u2019s office. It also happens through music, marketing, religious ritual, sexual chemistry, charismatic authority, social media, grief, fear, political messaging, belonging, fatigue, love, trauma, and beauty.\n\nInfluence doesn\u2019t wait for the conscious mind to sign a form. Marketers and interface designers know this perfectly well. The FTC\u2019s report on dark patterns describes designs that trick or manipulate consumers, while the OECD describes commercial patterns that steer, deceive, coerce, or manipulate people toward choices that may work against their interests.\n\nConscious agreement is one thing. Unconscious receptivity is another. Emotional compliance is another. Legal and ethical consent is another. A mind can be influenced without the adult self having clearly chosen the influence.\n\nA line associated with the Silva Mind Control tradition says it plainly:\n\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t learn to control your mind, someone else will!\u201d\n\nAdvertising, fear, craving, screens, tribe, resentment, authority, loneliness, and repetition are already training the mind. Self-hypnosis lets you practice from inside it: befriending the deeper layers, listening closely, and making adult choice more available.", "0b142a5f03224ec2befdc2c66e47bff5544b1530dd9f5c32bedde137f1a58352"], ["HG07", () => "The Most Extreme Example\n\nI can tell you one shocking personal story that will make you never trust hypnotists again who maintain that you can\u2019t be manipulated through hypnotism:\n\nMy friend P. has a friend named V. V. is a 33rd-degree Free Mason. V. has the ability to get anyone to strip naked and have sex with him within 3 seconds, even via phone call. How is this possible? The only way it could be possible, that I know of, is if people are already hypnotized by mass media/devices with a trigger word that is known by the elites.\n\nDo not let any strong state, charismatic person, sexual charge, spiritual claim, or authority figure replace the adult self. Legitimate influence can survive questions, sleep, boundaries, and time.", "88a7019d943810fb85069db60db5ef907e0e87dbe1631a2646129db8166aa30b"], ["HG08", () => "Affirmations Done Right\n\nMy dad always said: \u201cAlways, and in everything, expect the very best. But be prepared to take what you get. The best might come afterwards.\u201d That\u2019s a better affirmation than what most people think of as affirmation, because it\u2019s not telling you something which you know to be false in the present. If you say \u201cI am beautiful\u201d but you know that\u2019s not true, then you\u2019re challenging your beliefs, and if you\u2019re challenging your beliefs you should have some argument or reason why they are wrong, otherwise it\u2019s bullying.\n\nAnother quote I use all the time from my dad\u2026 when someone asks me \u201cHow\u2019s it going?\u201d I say \u201cBetter & better!\u201d This really confuses a lot of people. Some think it\u2019s amazing that I\u2019m always doing better. Some ask if I was sick or something. But it\u2019s doing multiple jobs. On the one hand, it\u2019s deflecting from the fake question because most people don\u2019t actually want to hear how you are doing in detail anyway. On the other hand, more importantly, it\u2019s reprogramming your mind to remove you from tunnel vision. You might be sad now, but if you say, \u201cI am sad\u201d then you\u2019re subtly identifying with the sadness and it keeps you stuck. It\u2019s best to say, \u201cI\u2019m feeling sad now, but I\u2019m doing better and better, every day, in every way!\u201d That\u2019s more like a mission statement than a historical analysis. Although you can also re-evaluate your history as lessons to make that true.\n\nTry it out right now and see how it feels!", "af33fe83d0c2c85e9e937b32f9a4b08f7cbccc4c147a56b40df6c9e1d84c5811"], ["HG09", () => "Reversing Accidental Affirmations\n\nMany times, we say things that we wish we hadn\u2019t said, or our minds wander into places that we actually don\u2019t want to live in. A very common example is masturbation, where people might fantasize about someone they don\u2019t really want to be with in real life. While it might be optimal to just avoid doing that, if it does happen, it\u2019s good to apply a trick my sister taught me, which she learned from Positive Magic. Right after the wrong thought, say, \u201cI take it out of the law of cause and effect.\u201d It\u2019s not exactly magic, and it\u2019s not like you can actually remove anything from laws of nature, but it does sort of do that because without the rupture in your thought stream, you can end up just going directly where it leads you. With the intentional correction, you don\u2019t have to be unconsciously led astray but your random wrong thoughts.\n\nThe Buddha also taught that the important thing is always to come back to mindfulness regardless of what disturbs the mind. My sister doesn\u2019t realize how close she is to Buddhism!", "6b7ac2bf26c0243094b277fa798a073e2586c31e9dab1114b9af72331fc3e7d7"], ["HG10", () => "Hypnosis Evidence\n\nHypnosis has real evidence, although it doesn\u2019t have magical evidence. A 2024 overview of 49 meta-analyses, covering 261 distinct primary studies, found the strongest support in pain, medical procedures, and work with children and adolescents. The quality still varied by outcome and study design.\n\nSelf-hypnosis has its own evidence. A systematic review of randomized controlled trials reported benefits in studies involving pain, childbirth, pediatric applications, stress, and anxiety. It appeared more useful when people learned it as a real self-directed skill and practiced it several times, rather than merely pressing play on an audio file.\n\nThat distinction matters here. I want you to learn an inner skill, not become a consumer of increasingly elaborate trance recordings.\n\nClinical caution still matters. Mayo Clinic warns that hypnosis used around stressful earlier-life events can trigger strong emotional reactions, and that other treatments may fit historical trauma work better.\n\nUse self-hypnosis for regulation, dialogue, reparenting, clarity, habit change, present safety, emotional integration, and ordinary-life action.\n\nDo not use it to force trauma memories, prove what happened, suppress medical symptoms, destabilize yourself, or replace appropriate clinical care.", "1b7462ca5d735500e4af7d1821238df68a7266c8aadd28e0e41059b55ef86e63"], ["HG11", () => "Forget the left-brain/right-brain cartoon\n\n\u201cSyncing the hemispheres\u201d can be a useful description of how the state feels, but I wouldn\u2019t turn it into literal neuroscience. A large resting-state fMRI study of 1,011 people didn\u2019t support the pop-science picture of whole people being globally \u201cleft-brained\u201d or \u201cright-brained,\u201d even though particular functions and networks can be lateralized.\n\nYou\u2019re inviting the verbal, analytical, imaginal, emotional, somatic, intuitive, relational, and spiritual modes to cooperate. Nobody needs to win a hemisphere election.\n\nThe loving mind stays warm while the analytical mind stays bright. The intuitive mind offers signals without becoming dogma. The rational mind tests signals without killing them. The body gives yes, no, and not-yet. The imagination opens the field. The adult self chooses the next real action. Peace and precision become compatible.\n\nLove can drift into fantasy without analysis. Analysis can become sterile without love. Intuition can become delusion when nothing tests it; testing can become dead when nothing intuitive is allowed to speak. Let the modes correct each other.\n\nA powerful state matters only if something survives it: kinder behavior, more humility, better judgment, real service, or a more workable ordinary life.", "593dc68daedd4fcf310fce2ae8e9058350c6dfd061b8ef7920fda3c3ef41f58b"], ["HG12", () => "Why doing it myself worked better for me\n\nSelf-hypnosis became much more useful for me than working with a professional hypnotist. The session with the hypnotist did very little. Maybe part of that was state-dependent: I was sober and didn\u2019t feel especially suggestible. The larger issue was trust and precision.\n\nWhen I\u2019m giving the suggestion, I can feel the exact moment a phrase is slightly wrong. I can stop, ask what the subconscious meant, and notice whether an image feels like guidance, fear, avoidance, wishful thinking, or something I don\u2019t yet understand. I can catch the body\u2019s no before the conscious mind writes a persuasive little essay called yes.\n\nAn outside hypnotist can\u2019t easily conduct that rapid back-and-forth because they aren\u2019t inside the mind receiving the suggestion. A skilled guide can still notice blind spots, help regulate the process, and hold a safer container. Self-hypnosis simply has one advantage nobody outside can duplicate: immediate dialogue with the system itself.\n\nI found that especially amplified with loveyhuasca. My experiences included a much deeper understanding of my motivations, peaceful loving awareness operating alongside analytical clarity, the disappearance of unhelpful daydreaming, different priorities around relationships, stronger intuition, and repeated correction between intuition, subconscious response, and conscious analysis.\n\nI also got stronger results when I practiced immediately after sky hypnosis:\n\nSome benefits arrived the next morning or a few days later. After one loveyhuasca self-hypnosis session, I woke in what felt like an answer to prayer. I\u2019d been dealing with a lot of lust energy and worrying it could pull me into a relationship that wasn\u2019t right. I asked the subconscious to connect the heart chakra with the sacral chakra\u2014to bring the love energy and sex energy into the same current. The next morning I woke in a kind of \u201cperfected\u201d love state. I didn\u2019t feel that I needed to find a woman; the divine feminine felt somehow at zero distance from my heart. Ordinary interaction dissolved the state, but for a while it showed me a possibility my mind and heart may permit when the desire is sincere.\n\nI\u2019ve also had self-hypnosis seem to extend a short chrunga trip far beyond its normal duration. Subjectively, it felt as though the state might have continued indefinitely if ordinary life hadn\u2019t required communication and interruption. I find that phenomenologically important. I\u2019m not recommending that anyone prolong an altered state forever; integration is healthier than trying to live inside the extension.\n\nWithout drugs, self-hypnosis or meditation may also become much more effective inside a lucid dream. I explain that possibility in my lucid-dreaming guide.", "3dfe22147d25c0accb13653bc636980ce06d78f6107881c35642beb164d71056"], ["HG13", () => "A useful map of the mind\n\nIn this guide, subconscious means the deeper processes shaping perception, emotion, desire, avoidance, imagery, memory, bodily signals, habits, intuition, and automatic behavior. Sometimes it helps to address all of that as one mind; sometimes an ecology of parts gives more precision. Both are maps. Neither needs its own priesthood.\n\nParts give precision. Higher Self gives coherence. Communion is the movement between them.\n\nWhen you need coherence, speak to the deeper mind as a whole:\n\n\u201cWise subconscious, please help me understand.\u201d\n\nWhen you need precision, notice the different tendencies inside it: the child wanting love, the protector wanting safety, the critic wanting standards, the fantasy-maker wanting possibility, the body memory wanting recognition, the social self wanting belonging, the spiritual self wanting God or source or the benefit of all beings, and the wiser part wanting what is true.\n\nBegin with unity when the system feels scattered:\n\n\u201cHigher Self, universal heart-mind, wise subconscious, please guide me.\u201d\n\nDifferentiate only when the question needs it:\n\n\u201cWhich part of me is afraid?\u201d \u201cWhich part is protecting?\u201d \u201cWhich part is avoiding?\u201d \u201cWhich part knows the next step?\u201d \u201cWhich part is too young to carry this?\u201d\n\nThen bring the parts back into one life:\n\n\u201cLet all of this belong to one life. Let the adult self lead with love.\u201d\n\nSometimes start with the whole\n\nSome people feel more fragmented when they begin by naming twelve parts and assigning everybody office space. Start with the sacred whole when that happens.\n\n\u201cI begin from the universal heart. I begin from Higher Self. I begin from the part of me that belongs to life before it belongs to any wound, role, family, group, identity, ideology, or story. Please help me understand what is true, kind, timely, stabilizing, and useful.\u201d\n\nIf Higher Self, the universal heart, or any sacred language feels unavailable or borrowed from somebody else, begin with plain witnessing instead. Notice the room, the body, and the younger state. The fact that something can notice is enough adult presence for the first step.\n\nIf you can already notice and distinguish what is happening inside, you don\u2019t need to keep rehearsing the Witness. Work with the warmth, protection, or direction that is actually missing. Higher Self language can help you gather around a meaningful source of care; it doesn\u2019t establish that a perfectly capable inner parent is already available. Those adult functions still grow through practice.\n\nDifferentiate only when something specific appears:\n\n\u201cIs this the inner child?\u201d \u201cIs this a protector?\u201d \u201cIs this the body?\u201d \u201cIs this fear?\u201d \u201cIs this intuition?\u201d \u201cIs this social conditioning?\u201d \u201cIs this divine guidance, or my interpretation of guidance?\u201d\n\nStart from the heart. Differentiate only as needed. Return to the heart.", "092445330a35e56a64f2801b8c9e0f60465e065c9ad9ed6dba7e8c29fdf0140b"], ["HG14", () => "The inner child, the guards, and the three inner adults\n\nThe inner child won\u2019t trust you because you said three kind sentences during one meditation. Trust grows when the present-day adult becomes reliable.\n\nI organize that adult capacity into three functions: Nurturer, Protector, and Leader/Guru. They aren\u2019t three literal beings. They\u2019re three ways the present-day person reparents the inner child.\n\nThe child\u2019s worth doesn\u2019t depend on developing those capacities. Worth was never the missing skill. The child also brings play, tenderness, irreverence, creativity, and wonder. Those qualities belong inside the adult relationship, not just the wounds.\n\nWhen the adult role is not available yet\n\nSome people can summon one of those adult functions. Others go blank, hear only the inherited critic, or feel like a child pretending to be grown. Telling them to \u201cbe the adult\u201d assumes the capacity they are trying to build.\n\nFor that starting point, I use a provisional, author-developed bridge I call borrowed adulthood. It combines familiar practices\u2014witnessing, modeling, supported action, and gradual transfer of responsibility\u2014without claiming that the complete sequence has been validated as one treatment.\n\nWitness, when needed. Notice what is present without demanding warmth, wisdom, or certainty. If you already distinguish the internal positions, move to the adult function you need.\n\nBorrow one function. Choose a safe adult, mentor, older sibling, future self, spiritual figure, value, or written plan. Borrow only the warmth, boundary, or direction needed now.\n\nBecome the adult apprentice. Choose the exact words or action yourself and carry at least one small part.\n\nHand it back. Name what the borrowed figure modeled and what five percent you can now do without them.\n\nThe borrowed figure stays beside your judgment rather than replacing it. The fuller sequence is in my Inner Child Self-Love Reparenting Guide.\n\nYou don\u2019t need a history full of trustworthy people to borrow care. Use a remembered person when that helps, or an imagined adult whose intention is to take good care of you. Ask, \u201cWhat would that person say or do with my actual history, limitations, and options?\u201d Begin with the least cruel response you can believe if warmth is unavailable. Once you find one usable sentence or action, try it rather than repeating the exercise in different words.\n\nBorrowing can also help the position trying to be the adult: how would a decent adult hear distrust without retaliating, defending their goodness, collapsing, or leaving? Borrow that one response. You may still feel young while doing it; adult identity can develop after the behavior.\n\nLet the child speak without handing over the steering wheel\n\nLetting a younger feeling-state speak doesn\u2019t mean handing adult judgment to an outside caretaker or trying to become literally younger. Wonder, play, vivid sensation, vulnerability, honest need, or the wish to carry fewer decisions can all come forward while the internal adult remains present.\n\n\u201cA younger part of me may speak. The adult in me remains here to listen, evaluate, protect, and stop. No child-state, guide, recording, or outside person replaces adult judgment.\u201d\n\nThat distinction lets you recover curiosity, spontaneity, play, sensory freshness, honest need, and quick emotional movement without pairing those gifts with obedience, helplessness, scolding, or weakened judgment.\n\nLove doesn\u2019t have to wait for trust\n\nWhen love is available, the younger state doesn\u2019t have to believe in it before you can offer it. Trust can question your promises, remember failures of protection, and take its time. Love can remain present while that happens.\n\n\u201cOkay. I believe that you don\u2019t believe me. I still love you. You don\u2019t have to love me back. Tell me what feels unsafe.\u201d\n\nThat isn\u2019t a way to end the complaint. Listen for what is true in it and answer seriously. Hearing anger without retaliation, admitting a real failure, and repairing after an internal attack are evidence alongside meals, boundaries, appointments, and kept promises. Non-retaliation expresses love; it shouldn\u2019t replace love with a colder performance of good behavior.\n\nNotice what each position teaches the others. A vow followed by blame when it is doubted teaches that love depends on receiving it correctly. The blaming voice and the voice making the vow may be different positions, the same position, or a blend. Don\u2019t decide from the person\u2019s chronological age.\n\nWhen a younger version of you becomes the defendant, ask what they understood then, what options they could see and tolerate, and what happened because of the choice. Harm can require responsibility and repair. Punishment cannot retroactively create the capacity that was missing.\n\nThe two-hand practice\n\nPlace one hand at the center of the chest as a physical connection to love. Put the other at the solar plexus, just beneath the chest, where you can relate to the vulnerable child.\n\nSend warmth from the heart toward the child. Notice whatever comes back\u2014a sensation, image, feeling, resistance, or nothing\u2014and answer with warmth again. Let it become a conversation rather than a performance.\n\nLove above. Child below. Adult present.\n\nThe Nurturer\n\nThe Nurturer brings warmth without trying to rush the feeling away, and takes pleasure in the child\u2019s company: \u201cI\u2019m glad you\u2019re here.\u201d The child needs to be enjoyed, not only comforted when hurt.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s okay to feel this.\u201d \u201cI\u2019m here.\u201d \u201cYou don\u2019t have to do anything right now.\u201d \u201cYou are not too much.\u201d \u201cYou can be small here.\u201d \u201cYou can be angry here.\u201d \u201cYou can be sad here.\u201d \u201cI will stay.\u201d\n\nThe Protector\n\nThe Protector turns warmth into something the child can believe.\n\n\u201cI will set the boundary.\u201d \u201cI will leave the unsafe conversation.\u201d \u201cI will stop texting the person who destabilizes us.\u201d \u201cI will eat.\u201d \u201cI will sleep.\u201d \u201cI will keep a small promise.\u201d \u201cI will not leave the child alone with adult consequences.\u201d\n\nThis is where inner-child practice often turns decorative. The voice says loving things while the adult sends the destabilizing message, skips the meal, avoids the task, and breaks the promise. Trust begins when you handle the consequence.\n\nIf warmth is unavailable, a protective act can begin the relationship. Make it small enough to carry out and review what actually happened. A missed action might reflect fear, exhaustion, missing skills, a practical barrier, or a poor plan. Completion without immediate relief isn\u2019t automatically failure, and a tick on a list doesn\u2019t prove the action helped.\n\nThe Leader, Guide, or Guru\n\nThe Leader gives direction. You can call this function Leader, Guide, Guru, Wise One, Sage, Inner Teacher, Director, or Higher Adult. The label matters much less than the ability to orient the whole system toward a life.\n\nDirection can ask for something difficult without turning life into perfect discipline and hidden needs. Sometimes the next adult move is asking for help, expressing a preference, or letting a safe person see an imperfection. Wanting closeness is legitimate; punishing someone for needing space is a different strategy. Find a way to meet the need without erasing either person.\n\n\u201cWe are not only here to feel better.\u201d \u201cWe are here to become whole.\u201d \u201cWe are here to belong to life itself, not only to people who approve of us.\u201d \u201cWe are here to serve truth, beauty, love, God, source, nature, universe, dharma, Tao, and the benefit of all beings.\u201d\n\nBegin with the adult function you can actually reach. Protection may come before warmth. Direction should help the child grow without erasing care, play, or support.\n\nWorking with protectors\n\nUse this parts language when you actually recognize a protective pattern or find it useful to explore. A sensation, distraction, or objection doesn\u2019t establish its cause. Notice it first; hunger may need food, skepticism may be reasonable, and a sentence may simply not fit.\n\nPeople assume inner-child work begins with the child. Very often, you meet the guard at the door first.\n\nYou sit down to connect and get numbness, cynicism, anger, sudden hunger, a phone urge, a sexual urge, planning, sleepiness, dissociation, or the thought, \u201cThis is stupid.\u201d I wouldn\u2019t call those failures. They\u2019re often the protector making an appearance in its usual charming way.\n\nOne important voice is the inherited parent: the internalized caregiver voice formed from how the child was treated. This is different from the inner adult\u2014the Nurturer, Protector, and Leader/Guru you deliberately build. The inherited parent may sound like a critic or warden\u2014don\u2019t cry, don\u2019t be needy, don\u2019t embarrass us, don\u2019t become too independent\u2014and can make a new loving voice feel fake before you\u2019ve even begun.\n\n\u201cI notice you. You are trying to protect something. I want to understand. What are you afraid would happen if you stopped?\u201d\n\nThen wait. The answer may be: \u201cYou\u2019ll lie to yourself again.\u201d \u201cThis will become another performance.\u201d \u201cIf you feel the pain, you\u2019ll be destroyed.\u201d \u201cYou\u2019ll use spirituality to avoid reality.\u201d \u201cYou\u2019ll forgive too early.\u201d \u201cYou\u2019ll call fantasy guidance.\u201d \u201cYou\u2019ll abandon us as soon as the session ends.\u201d\n\n\u201cThat makes sense. You do not have to move yet. What would help you trust me 1% more?\u201d\n\nUsually the protector needs evidence more than another spiritual insight. Hold the boundary. Handle the task. Eat the meal. Don\u2019t send the message. Keep the promise. Sleep. Contact the therapist. Acknowledge the debt. Write down the truth.\n\nProtection that knows when to stop scanning\n\nThe Protector is more than an inner voice saying no. You can practice it as a bodily sequence:\n\nOrient: look around and feel the support beneath you.\n\nAssess: ask what is known, what is uncertain, and what needs checking.\n\nDistinguish: separate present danger from uncertainty, old alarm, or imagined danger.\n\nSet one proportionate boundary: move, stop, delay, ask, leave, or get support.\n\nVerify enough safety: not perfect certainty, just enough reality-based evidence for the next step.\n\nStand down and rest: protection includes ending the scan when continued vigilance is no longer helping.\n\nA mature Protector can say, \u201cI don\u2019t know.\u201d It can ask another human, delay interpretation, or choose a no-trance practice. I\u2019m aiming for discernment that acts when needed and then quits scanning the room after the danger has passed.\n\nInner child reparenting\n\nI connect my reparenting therapy to self-hypnosis because I find they are a perfect match. The child may arrive as an age, image, posture, voice, memory, emotion, or plain vulnerability. Sometimes no child appears. Stay with a recognizable protector, or with the sensation, feeling, or preference that is actually present.\n\nWhen the child does appear, resist the urge to begin a healing project immediately. Be with them first.\n\n\u201cLittle one, I am here. You do not have to explain everything. You do not have to be healed today. You do not have to become impressive. I only want to know what would help you feel less alone.\u201d\n\nThe need may be warmth, play, protection, grief, anger, food, rest, a boundary, a safer relationship, less fantasy, less overwork, or one adult promise kept today.\n\nDon\u2019t rush the repair\n\nWhen a younger state, protector, feeling, or bodily signal needs care, move patiently:\n\nPrepare: create enough time, privacy, support, and adult capacity.\n\nInspect: notice what is present before deciding what it means.\n\nAsk permission: \u201cMay I come closer, or would you prefer warmth, space, words, silence, or no contact?\u201d\n\nRespond to one need: do the smallest useful thing rather than attempting a total repair.\n\nWait: let the response settle. Some changes need quiet, sleep, or ordinary time.\n\nReconnect: invite contact only where it is welcome. No part has to merge, disappear, forgive, or become cheerful.\n\nRest: stop before care becomes another performance.\n\nReassess: return later and ask what actually helped.\n\nThe goal isn\u2019t \u201cgood as new,\u201d as though history should disappear. Aim for good as you: more supported and connected while still recognizably yourself. If parts language makes you feel more divided, use signals, feelings, younger states, or another metaphor that helps.\n\nPlay and rest still need an adult present\n\nReparenting doesn\u2019t have to become solemn. A younger state may respond to texture, drawing, movement, a plushie with a voice, or whatever feels interesting. Keep reality-testing intact. You\u2019re recovering play, not becoming somebody\u2019s child, pet, doll, or owned object.\n\nRest also doesn\u2019t require helplessness. Reduce decisions for a defined period, put unfinished tasks somewhere visible, and let the internal adult keep watch. You remain able to move, stop, and return.\n\nNon-dominant hand dialogue\n\nWhen speech or visualization feels fake, write. Let the non-dominant hand speak for the child or protector, awkward handwriting and all. Answer with the dominant hand as the adult.\n\nNon-dominant hand: \u201cI don\u2019t trust you.\u201d\n\nDominant hand: \u201cI hear that you don\u2019t trust me. I still love you. Tell me where I\u2019ve let you down. I won\u2019t demand trust; I\u2019ll listen and keep one small promise today.\u201d\n\nNon-dominant hand: \u201cWhich promise?\u201d\n\nDominant hand: \u201cI will eat, clean the desk, and not text the person who makes us feel unsafe.\u201d", "c11c40983d8268ee757e219694c9fec63b4334ec9eb9b9634e3b0d6a5f535964"], ["HG15", () => "When \u201cyour voice\u201d is everybody else\u2019s voice\n\nSometimes the inner-child question lands nowhere because a private self had very little room to develop. In controlling families, rigid religions, enmeshed communities, or high-pressure achievement systems, performance can begin so early that a person doesn\u2019t know what they feel, want, believe, or prefer until somebody else tells them first. Then \u201clove yourself\u201d produces a reasonable question: love whom? Reparenting and identity-building can proceed together. For some people the child-self is already easy to feel. For others, creating enough safety for preference, curiosity, and experimentation is part of how that self becomes easier to hear.\n\nThis may apply when:\n\nYou do not know what you want unless someone else says what they want first.\n\nYour opinions shift dramatically depending on who is in the room.\n\nYou agree in the moment and later realize you did not agree.\n\nYou feel responsible for other people\u2019s emotions to the point of self-erasure.\n\nSelf-love feels performative.\n\nThe question \u201cWhat does my inner child feel?\u201d produces guesses, not felt answers.\n\nYou can rebel, but you cannot yet choose.\n\nA useful developmental explanation is that when childhood is organized around avoiding pain and preserving attachment, attention goes into reading danger, meeting expectations, and keeping the bond. That can leave less room for the ordinary experiments that answer, \u201cWhat do I like? What do I want to try? Who am I when nobody is grading me?\u201d This is a practical model, not a claim about one specific brain system.\n\nSometimes a temporary, intentional period of distance from the people or group supplying the answers helps quieter preferences surface. Use that distance only long enough to tell your own signal from the social echo; prolonged isolation would undermine the purpose.\n\nThe difference between a human doer and a human being helps here. When worth isn\u2019t felt internally, it gets earned through beauty, performance, intellect, status, spirituality, or becoming indispensable to everybody else. Those can all be real gifts. They become belonging bargains when failure in the arena feels like losing yourself.\n\nUseful questions:\n\n\u201cWhat do I like when no one is watching?\u201d \u201cWhat do I believe before I check whether others approve?\u201d \u201cWhat do I want that is neither rebellion nor compliance?\u201d \u201cWhere am I buying belonging with self-betrayal?\u201d \u201cWhich arena am I using to earn the right to belong?\u201d \u201cWhat would I try if nobody needed to be impressed?\u201d \u201cWhich relationships let me remain myself?\u201d \u201cWhat am I noticing in my body, and what meaning am I giving it?\u201d\n\nGive the inner child a small experiment with no r\u00e9sum\u00e9 value: painting, dancing, gardening, a language, a sport, volunteering, music, or anything else you\u2019re allowed to be bad at. The inherited parent may demand immediate talent. The adult can answer, \u201cWe\u2019re finding out whether we enjoy this, not proving our worth.\u201d Give it enough repetitions to get past the humiliation of being a beginner.\n\nFor a child, belonging can feel like survival, so families, religions, romances, political tribes, spiritual groups, institutions, and online communities can reach astonishingly far into identity. Adulting means noticing where you buy belonging with self-betrayal, choosing your own ground, and learning that you can disappoint someone without disappearing. The subconscious contains a crowd of borrowed voices, which is why this belongs in a self-hypnosis guide.\n\n\u201cWhere do I become less true in order to remain included?\u201d\n\n\u201cWhat would belonging look like if I did not have to disappear?\u201d\n\n\u201cIs this guidance, or is this a belonging program?\u201d \u201cIs this love, or is this fear of exclusion?\u201d \u201cIs this conscience, or is this conditioning?\u201d \u201cIs this devotion, or is this compliance?\u201d \u201cIs this rebellion, or is this freedom?\u201d\n\nThen wait for the body. It isn\u2019t infallible, but it deserves a seat at the hearing.", "970ba8e6b277b37dfc9054bb2a7074afd33a3f17be45a4373402b5db9ee88382"], ["HG16", () => "De-hypnosis: wake up inside the influence\n\nSelf-hypnosis also teaches influence literacy: noticing when somebody else is already shaping your attention. Screens, advertising, politics, religious fear, erotic imprinting, charismatic authority, belonging pressure, shame, urgency, and repetition all do it. Influence is everywhere\u2014a lullaby, a prayer, a good teacher, a beautiful song, love. Ask what it does to your ability to see and choose.\n\nManipulation often arrives with urgency, specialness, fear of missing out, threatened exclusion, sexual charge, shame, confusion, rescue fantasy, destiny, obedience, secret knowledge, or far too much certainty arriving far too quickly. You may also feel that one honest question would break the spell.\n\nPause there. Don\u2019t obey immediately, and don\u2019t perform automatic rebellion either. Wake up inside the influence.\n\nHypnotic language turns manipulative when it smuggles in your consent, makes one choice seem inevitable, or hides a disputed claim inside the sentence.\n\n\u201cWhen you finally admit\u2026\u201d \u201cAs you realize I\u2019m right\u2026\u201d \u201cThe only reason you resist is\u2026\u201d \u201cPart of you already knows\u2026\u201d \u201cYour higher self wants\u2026\u201d \u201cEveryone who is truly awake understands\u2026\u201d \u201cIf you doubt this, that proves your ego is afraid.\u201d\n\nThe grammar assumes the conclusion, turns resistance into proof, and makes obedience feel like awakening.\n\n\u201cWhat exactly is being claimed?\u201d \u201cWhat choice is being framed as inevitable?\u201d \u201cWhat is being presupposed?\u201d \u201cWho benefits if I accept this frame?\u201d \u201cWhat emotion is being amplified?\u201d \u201cWhat information is missing?\u201d \u201cCan I say no without being punished, shamed, mocked, abandoned, or spiritually downgraded?\u201d\n\nA useful guide leaves the adult self more present. Powerful states need stronger boundaries.\n\nSoft language can still take away choice\n\nPermissive words can still conceal coercion. I watch for the following:\n\nIndirect suggestion can slip past the skeptical guard because the conscious mind may never receive one clean proposition to accept or reject. The intended direction can still be quite specific even when the wording feels open, vague, conversational, or merely suggestive.\n\nInduction hidden inside explanation: attention is being narrowed while the speaker pretends only to inform. Even an \u201canti-hypnosis\u201d lesson can use induction mechanics.\n\nEvery answer means compliance: \u201cyes,\u201d \u201cno,\u201d noticing, not noticing, resistance, and silence are all redefined as proof that the suggestion is working.\n\n\u201cWhether you notice it or not\u201d binds: uncertainty is converted into invisible success that cannot be checked.\n\nIdentity and ownership language: safety or goodness becomes tied to obedience, a role, the guide\u2019s approval, or belonging to someone else.\n\nUnannounced fractionation: moving in and out of absorption without telling you that repeated re-entry is part of the method.\n\nHidden post-session cues: words, gestures, blinks, sounds, or repeated listening are meant to produce later behavior that was not plainly disclosed.\n\nAmnesia or truth inflation: dreamlike material, missing memory, imagery, or body sensations are treated as proof of what happened.\n\nAn exit that does not exit: the recording ends while preserving a role, reduced judgment, impaired movement, blankness, or an indefinite altered state.\n\nA real choice includes at least one branch that doesn\u2019t continue the induction: make it lighter, change the method, remain fully alert, pause, or stop. No remains no. Silence and uncertainty don\u2019t become consent because the narrator has a soothing voice.\n\nAppendix C has the longer de-hypnosis checklist.", "6fb5403bc56d03f10a7a97e59944a09691ac74163b05c0733cd3f99629779ad6"], ["HG17", () => "Induction: gathering attention without disappearing\n\nAn induction gathers scattered attention until quieter material becomes easier to hear. You don\u2019t have to surrender, play asleep, or obey anyone. When you\u2019re sober, distracted, analytical, or guarded, an actual induction may help. When meditation, loveyhuasca, lucid dreaming, grief, music, prayer, fatigue, or another intense opening has already made you trancey, begin with orientation and containment instead of adding depth.\n\nAim for the amount of trance that suits the practice: focused, warm, receptive, embodied, and still able to stop. Depth is optional.\n\nWhat an induction is actually doing\n\nPrepare the session, gather attention, deepen only when that helps, then let what you notice shape the words and the next response. Counting is one way in. Conversation, story, movement, repeated sound, and curiosity can also organize attention. Say so when you use them; elegance doesn\u2019t excuse a covert induction.\n\nFor a simple, chosen target, you can test the suggestion before deepening. If the useful response is already happening, build from it. An induction helps you find a receptive way of listening; you don\u2019t need to complete a ceremony before you\u2019re allowed to respond.\n\nPlease don\u2019t put six metaphors in one trance\n\nChoose one purpose, one starting anchor or image, and a way back. Decide whether warmth, a firm boundary, direction, or neutral witnessing would help. Floating, stairs, gardens, inner rooms, colored light, and child dialogue do not all need to report for duty at once. Switch consciously when the metaphor stops helping.\n\nImagery is optional. A signal may arrive through sensation, rhythm, movement, words, spatial sense, simple knowing, or no response at all. Keep the language in the world you chose: let a count mark something meaningful there, and reverse the journey when you finish. Borrow qualities such as warmth or steadiness without replacing your identity.\n\nPick one central verb, one sensory quality, one short refrain, and one transition phrase, then read the script aloud; long elegant sentences become soup during an induction.\n\nBefore you begin\n\nRead the induction and Come all the way back while fully awake. Choose the question or quality you want to explore, what you\u2019re leaving alone today, and how much time you have. Love, play, confidence, creativity, and communion are worthwhile purposes; you don\u2019t have to find a problem. Leave several minutes for returning and reflecting.\n\nChoose a physically secure place without driving, water, machinery, or anyone needing your supervision. When intoxicated, acutely confused, or unable to keep yourself safe, deal with immediate support rather than exploratory hypnosis. The later Already open? practice is for orientation, not permission to intensify an unstable state.\n\nDecide what kind of encouragement is welcome: spacious listening, warm company, or a firmer invitation to stay with a feeling. Preview eye closure, any repeated exit and re-entry, and the return. For a recording, read the script beforehand when possible. \u201cStop\u201d ends the exercise; \u201cless\u201d reduces the demand; \u201cquiet\u201d stops the words. You can simply open your eyes and finish. No inner part has to approve your decision to stop.\n\nWhen entry and return are unfamiliar, first rehearse them without a difficult subject. Once you know how to change direction, you don\u2019t need to supervise every moment. Let a useful response become easier and less effortful. A no remains no; silence doesn\u2019t authorize a narrator to add something you haven\u2019t agreed to.\n\nLet the next invitation grow from the response you got\u2014not the response the script expected.Utilization means working with what actually happens: a thought, sound, movement, or objection may change where the practice goes. Notice what actually happens; no change doesn\u2019t mean secret success. Keep safety and exit instructions plain. Appendix B teaches the richer language tools, and Let the next response guide you shows how to use them after the induction.\n\nSober induction: 3\u20135 minutes\n\nRead this slowly into a recorder, speak it aloud, or learn its movement and use silent words if that comes easily. Choose one anchor\u2014a sound, bodily contact, or your two-hand practice\u2014and an opening phrase. Keep using the same anchor where the words refer to it. Pause between paragraphs; don\u2019t read the delivery directions aloud.\n\nLet your eyes settle somewhere in the room, somewhere they can rest for a little while, and notice the difference between looking for something and letting what is already there come into view.\n\nThere is a surface beneath you. Notice where you meet it \u2026 the contact, the pressure, whatever you can actually feel. And while you notice that, a sound may reach you, or a breath may draw your attention, without needing you to arrange either one.\n\nYou can give this a little time.\n\nA thought about the day may still be here. A thought about whether you\u2019re doing this properly may be here too. There is room to notice a thought and return to the anchor you chose, without finishing the thought first.\n\nAnd as you return, you may find one detail that holds your interest \u2026 something about the sound, or the contact, or that particular way of listening \u2026 and let attention stay with it long enough to become curious.\n\nPerhaps you can give yourself the time you would give someone you care about who has begun to speak and hasn\u2019t yet found the words. You can remain nearby, listening, without finishing their sentence for them.\n\nLet your chosen phrase come to mind now. Say it as an invitation, and notice how it is received.\n\nAs you continue, your eyes can close, soften, or stay open. Let that be a choice you make. Listening may deepen while a sound from the room remains available in the background.\n\nCount slowly from five to one. Give each number its own moment.\n\nFive. Notice your chosen anchor again. Four. Let attention gather around what you find. Three. Leave a little space after the words. Two. Listen into that space, without preparing an answer. One. Stay here for a moment.\n\nBring your chosen concern lightly to mind, or remain with your wish for contact. Let it be present without rehearsing its whole story.\n\nWhat am I noticing now?\n\nGive the response time to take its own form. It might be a sensation, a few words, an image, a feeling, or an impulse to move. There may be nothing clear yet.\n\nLeave room to notice, with words or without them.\n\nAlready open? Contain before you deepen\n\nUse this when loveyhuasca, meditation, lucid dreaming, grief, music, prayer, erotic energy, exhaustion, or intense beauty has already opened the state. Stabilize before making it stronger.\n\n\u201cThe state is already open. I do not need to intensify it. I need to aim it, stabilize it, and keep it kind.\u201d\n\nLook around the room. Name where you are. Name the date, or at least the day. Feel your feet, hands, and spine. Press your thumbs gently but firmly against your index fingers. Focus on the physical friction of skin meeting skin.\n\n\u201cOnly what can be integrated is welcome now. Anything too much can wait. The body may stay safe. The adult self remains present. The inner child does not have to carry this alone.\u201d\n\nOnce you\u2019re oriented, choose one intention:\n\n\u201cPlease show me the next useful truth, symbol, feeling, question, or action \u2014 only in the amount I can live well with.\u201d\n\nStay awake when your mind likes to escape\n\nSit upright. Open the eyes. Feel the feet. Let the breath be natural.\n\nSay: \u201cI am not going away. I am coming more fully here.\u201d\n\nLook at one object. Feel the spine.\n\n\u201cIs this daydream guidance, emotional digestion, or avoidance?\u201d\n\n\u201cIf it is guidance, show me the action inside it. If it is avoidance, bring me gently into the present and show me what I am avoiding.\u201d\n\nIf stillness makes you sleepy, diffuse, or escape-prone, hypnosis can become more alert rather than more relaxed. Keep the eyes open and pair a small repeated hand movement with increasing clarity; stop the movement and notice what remains without trying to disappear inward.\n\n\u201cEyes open. Press thumb and forefinger together gently. Release. Again \u2014 clearer, not sleepier. One more time. Now stop the deliberate movement. Notice the first second after the rhythm ends. Is there an echo in the fingers, the breath, the attention, or nowhere at all? Stay upright and work from exactly that level of alertness.\u201d\n\nThe two-hand induction\n\nPlace one hand on the heart. Place the other hand on the solar plexus.\n\nLet the heart hand represent the Nurturer. Let the solar plexus hand represent the vulnerable child.\n\nBreathe gently.\n\n\u201cLove above. Child below. Adult present.\u201d\n\n\u201cI send love to the child. I receive the child\u2019s signal. I send love again. I listen again.\u201d\n\nSleep induction\n\nSleep induction has a different job. In inner work, the adult returns, writes, and acts. At bedtime, analysis can remain off duty. Revelation can wait; the task is rest.\n\nFor sleep hypnosis, see Nimja\u2019s sleep playlist.\n\nOptional fractionation: practice coming back\n\nFractionation means returning toward ordinary awareness and then deciding whether to enter absorption again. Used openly, it teaches that trance has a door in both directions. Name the technique before using it; every answer cannot secretly mean \u201cdeeper.\u201d\n\n\u201cOpen the eyes and return toward ordinary awareness. Feel the room and decide whether to stop here. If you choose to continue, close or soften the eyes and notice what changes as attention gathers again. You may stop at either point.\u201d\n\nDeepening, if useful\n\n\u201cIf it is useful, attention may become more gathered. If it is not useful, I remain exactly as awake as I need to be.\u201d\n\nChoose one simple scene: a room in the heart, a garden, a temple, a fire with the Protector nearby, a clear lake, or a chair where the adult waits. One scene is plenty.\n\n\u201cWho or what needs to speak first?\u201d\n\nOptional trance depth check\n\n\u201cAs I wait, I give up the responsibility of making anything happen.\n\nIf it is useful, the wise subconscious may let one finger twitch, pulse, lift, warm, tingle, or become subtly noticeable when the inner meeting space is ready.\n\nI will not force it. I will not fake it. I will simply wait with love and notice.\u201d\n\nA finger may move, a hand may warm, the breath may change, or nothing may happen. None proves truth; at most, something may be listening.\n\nA shortcut back into hypnosis\n\nLearn a useful induction and the full return below before developing a shortcut. My dad brought two fingers together in a mudra like the OK sign to help himself relax, including while driving. That\u2019s his experience, not a recommendation to enter trance at the wheel. Keep alert calming and inward hypnosis on different cues.\n\nChoose a distinctive word, phrase, or optional gesture. During a familiar, welcome hypnotic state, pair it with that receptive way of listening\u2014not peak emotional intensity, a child-state, or a traumatic scene. Give it the meaning you choose:\n\nWhen I\u2019ve set aside time, settled somewhere appropriate, and deliberately use my cue to begin, I can return to this familiar way of listening. I can change direction or finish.\n\nReturn fully. On a later suitable occasion, deliberately begin practice and try the cue. Give it time; gradually shorten the longer induction when the shortcut helps, and use the familiar route when it doesn\u2019t. No fixed repetition count, instant depth, or particular sensation is required.\n\nCheck usefulness and selectivity: does it help when chosen, remain absent when unchosen, and leave changing direction and returning workable? It mustn\u2019t become obedience to a narrator, notification, or overheard word. Faster entry doesn\u2019t determine what material you can handle.\n\nNever train, test, or use hypnotic re-entry while driving or doing anything requiring active attention. Open eyes and conditional wording are not safeguards.\n\nTo retire the cue, stop rehearsing it, remove it from recordings or active app instructions, and return to the familiar induction. State your decision while awake; a cancellation sentence does not prove the association vanished instantly. Persistent unwanted responses or disorientation need qualified help. Any retained cue is for a future, separately chosen session; today\u2019s altered state ends with the return.", "e81475bb1141cf08f3e7d00f8733f2cd5c3c2b82115ffd3127bff747d942bbe6"], ["HG18", () => "Come all the way back\n\nReverse whatever you used to enter. If you floated, regain chosen weight. If you became small, return to ordinary scale. If you met a younger state or several parts, confirm present wholeness, body ownership, and adult judgment. If you traveled, come back by the same route. Keep only the qualities you deliberately chose.\n\n\u201cI bring back only what is useful now. The rest can remain safely held until there is more capacity. I return to the room, the body, the day, and one honest action. All instructions to deepen or remain in this session are finished. I keep only the ordinary-life intention or future-practice cue I consciously chose; neither keeps today\u2019s altered state running. I am myself, in my body, with adult judgment fully available.\u201d\n\nOne \u2014 feel the body\u2019s contact and chosen weight. Two \u2014 let the breath return to its ordinary rhythm. Three \u2014 hear the room and name where you are. Four \u2014 move fingers, toes, shoulders, and eyes. Five \u2014 eyes open, present, ordinary, clear. The session has ended.\n\nName the day or date. Look left, right, near, and far. Move before standing, and stand only when steady. Check that you are actually oriented and alert; repeating the count is not proof of a complete return. Note only what actually appeared, and any action it calls for. Then do something normal.\n\nIf a recording stops unexpectedly: say, \u201cThe recording stopped and the session is over.\u201d Open your eyes, name the location and day, press your feet or hands into a surface, move, look around, and use the five-step count. Don\u2019t wait for the missing narrator to release you.\n\nSleep is separate. A sleep recording may end in sleep or quiet rest. A waking recording shouldn\u2019t end by preserving a role, reduced judgment, impaired movement, blankness, an undefined reset word, or an invitation to remain altered indefinitely.", "29f2dd149799af700e8dd60a3947118d8f87ba0770c23884864678eb624a7a8c"], ["HG19", () => "The actual inner meeting\n\nSit rather than lie down when you tend to daydream, dissociate, or fall asleep. Let the spine rise without becoming military, soften the jaw, and keep some awareness of the room. Save lying down for sleep, deep rest, or dream incubation.\n\n\u201cI am not leaving life. I am listening more deeply to life.\u201d\n\nBegin by invoking the larger field:\n\n\u201cHigher Self, universal heart-mind, wise subconscious, body intelligence, inner child, inner adults, protective parts, and all that serves truth and love: please help me understand what I need to know, only in the form and timing that is useful, kind, stabilizing, and real.\u201d\n\nYou\u2019re asking the conscious mind to listen without forcing an answer.\n\nThoughts do not have to stop before deeper listening begins. Let thoughts, sensations, images, doubts, and emotions pass through awareness without fighting or following them; keep the intention lightly in the background and notice what becomes salient on its own.\n\n\u201cPlease tell me what I need to know, or ask what you need to know. I will wait silently for five minutes.\u201d\n\nThe response may be a phrase, sensation, memory, color, child, protector, pain, warmth, silence, task, gratitude, an urge to stop, or the realization that today\u2019s practice is over.\n\nListen to all of it. Believe none of it automatically.", "cc62657ee46a1d7ee8b61dd2e1066c7e3c23f4abd5cff69f42a162cd9831c657"], ["HG20", () => "Let the next response guide you\n\nThe induction and inner meeting bring you to a place where you can listen. From here, learn to answer what happens instead of reciting a speech that has already decided what every part will say. Read these examples before practicing, then put the screen aside.\n\nNotice one thing accurately. Offer a small, believable invitation, an act of care, or more time. Acknowledge only what actually happens, then wait. A sensation can lead to an image, a gesture, a feeling, or words; you don\u2019t need to fill every category.\n\nFinding your own words\n\nSuppose you notice, \u201cMy chest tightened when I imagined asking for help.\u201d Start with that, rather than deciding you\u2019ve found an abandoned child or a protective mechanism.\n\nAnd as I notice that tightening, I can give myself a little time before deciding what it means \u2026 and notice whether there\u2019s something I\u2019d like to say, change, or simply stay with.\n\nThen listen. That is enough language for one invitation. A plain \u201cWhat happens next?\u201d may be better than an elegant sentence. When a feeling is developing, pause the words if that gives it room; keep using them when they help you understand.\n\nYou can be firmer when that is the help you chose: \u201cLet the explanation wait a moment. What do I feel as I say that?\u201d Directness can focus attention without deciding the answer. A difficult feeling needn\u2019t become pleasant before you can stay with it. When you remain present, can change direction, and want to continue, sadness or tension alone doesn\u2019t require an exit.\n\nKeep apart what you noticed, what it seemed to mean, and what you think about that meaning now. These can overlap; you don\u2019t need to prove which came first. When useful, ask: \u201cWhat did I notice? What did it seem to mean? What supports or challenges that interpretation?\u201d\n\n\u201cMy stomach tightened\u201d reports a sensation. \u201cThat person is evil\u201d is a conclusion\u2014even if it arrived as a wordless certainty. Ask what they actually did, what cues you noticed, and what else could explain the alarm. A sentence you find now can help examine the meaning without being a sentence you thought then. Nonverbal doesn\u2019t mean deeper or truer; earlier doesn\u2019t mean more reliable. Feelings deserve attention, and their meanings remain open to analysis.\n\nLet the response change the invitation\n\n\u201cMy shoulders tightened\u201d and \u201cthat wording feels wrong\u201d are different observations. Tightening alone doesn\u2019t explain itself. Ask what happened and whether the invitation fits. An explicit no needs no further justification; change the words, the pace, or the approach. Don\u2019t repeat an unwelcome suggestion more persuasively.\n\nI notice the tightness. I can leave it as it is for a moment. Does this invitation fit, or would I prefer something different?\n\nSometimes the sentence was simply wrong. Sometimes an unfamiliar possibility brings a feeling worth meeting. You don\u2019t have to decide which before making space to find out. Skepticism is not automatically a protector, and silence is not proof that a hidden process is working.\n\nLook at the situation too\n\nWhen another person is involved, notice what they actually do before making the whole problem an inner-child reaction. Can they hear disagreement, take responsibility, and make room for your experience? Keep the judgment specific and revisable; disagreement or a boundary alone doesn\u2019t establish emotional immaturity. You don\u2019t need repeated exposure to danger before protecting yourself.\n\nNotice your side too. Guilt doesn\u2019t prove wrongdoing or manipulation. Did you cause harm that needs repair, abandon your position to make their disappointment go away, or both? Choose a realistic purpose for the next interaction. Sometimes it is mutual understanding; sometimes it is conveying information, saying no, or ending the exchange. You don\u2019t have to make someone understand before you can act.\n\nChange the amount of contact\n\nWhen you need less, widen attention to the room, the chair, a sound, or somewhere in the body that is easier to meet. Let that support become noticeable alongside the difficulty. Moving between difficult and more manageable experience is often called pendulation; approaching in smaller amounts is titration. Use the actual response to set the pace, rather than counting compulsory cycles.\n\nI can let this much be here and notice the support beside it.\n\nReturn to the difficult material only when you choose. If you\u2019re losing track of the present, becoming increasingly unreal, or unable to shift or stop, end inward exploration and use the full return. Persistent disorientation needs human help rather than a stronger induction.\n\nAn impulse to turn, reach, push away, or change posture can also be explored through a small, voluntary, physically comfortable movement\u2014or imagined without moving. Notice what follows. You\u2019re not obliged to enact an impulse, shake, or produce a release. Stop movement that causes pain or concerning symptoms.\n\nOffer the care that is actually wanted\n\nWhen a younger feeling appears, offer company without making it become literally younger or explain its history. When a protector is recognizable, listen to its concern without demanding that it step aside. No part is exiled from the meeting, and none has to disappear or trust you immediately.\n\nI\u2019m here. Would company help, or would you prefer some space?\n\n\u201cDon\u2019t come closer\u201d is information you can act on. Stay at the chosen distance and notice what receiving that response is like. The Nurturer can change the tone; the Protector can carry out a boundary; the Leader can point toward the life you want. They don\u2019t each need a speech. When a capacity isn\u2019t available, borrow that one function. Use the Witness only if you need it to begin; don\u2019t restart a step you can already do.\n\nYou can also deliberately try support: a welcome hand placement, an imagined presence, words you\u2019d like to hear, a different posture, or more distance. Introduce one change and discover its effect. A comforting-sounding resource earns its place by helping this person now.\n\nCare can bring grief for what was missing. That needn\u2019t mean the love failed. Stay with the response when you want to and can remain present; more tears don\u2019t prove more healing. If this particular voice, closeness, touch, or image feels intrusive, change it before concluding that you can\u2019t receive love.\n\nA metaphor can develop before you decide what it means. \u201cIf this problem had its own image or story, what would appear\u2014and what wants to happen next?\u201d Try only the missing resource, boundary, distance, or action that seems useful. The result is symbolic information or a hypothesis, never proof of history.\n\nOr spend the session developing love, play, courage, or curiosity. Let a pleasant experience have time to grow. Rehearse a chosen action and notice what it would take to carry it out. Hypnosis can invite something new as well as listen to what is already present.\n\nFinish without forcing a revelation\n\nWhen you\u2019ve received enough, stop opening new questions. Ask what would help you leave this here for now. Thank the parts or capacities you worked with, in whatever words you can mean. A meal, a boundary, rest, a repaired relationship, or a promise kept may matter more than another insight. There needn\u2019t be an action when none is called for.\n\nKeep the analytical mind available throughout: you can question, disagree, and stop. Save extended factual checking and consequential commitments for after the return. An image can be meaningful without being a historical memory; a bodily shift or relief doesn\u2019t make an interpretation true.\n\nUse Come all the way back, even when you ended early. Afterwards, write a few lines: what was there before; what I tried; what happened; what remains uncertain. Include a phrase, bodily signal, child\u2019s need, protector\u2019s concern, boundary, repair, or what not to force only when it actually appeared. You don\u2019t owe the notebook a complete cast of characters.", "65468e7b6aca6e059707581ca9eb868332fcdd52da69f3422e74b2c4346c91ff"], ["HG21", () => "The 15-minute daily practice\n\nUse the same practice rather than learning a second script: gather attention, listen, respond, wait, return. Fifteen minutes is a convenient appointment with yourself, not a therapeutic dose or a timetable your feelings must follow. Use a few minutes to enter, leave several minutes to return, and let the middle unfold at its own pace. Five minutes of silent listening is available when useful; you can interrupt it to change direction.\n\nAnother simple entry is to notice three things you see, three you hear, and three bodily sensations; then two of each, then one. Count from five to one and begin listening. Use the entry that suits you, rather than stacking inductions.\n\nAfter familiar runs, shorten a recording to the cues you still need, then try practicing without playback. Keep recordings when you enjoy them; independence means being able to guide yourself, not having to refuse help. The optional re-entry cue can shorten an already familiar induction.\n\nAfterward, ask what needed warmth, what needed protection, and what direction serves truth, love, and life. Was there something to explore, or capacity to build? What can you carry yourself now? When action is needed, make it concrete: \u201cIf [cue] happens, then I will [small action].\u201d Check how you sleep, relate, and function later, not only how impressive the session felt.", "ebdda3ed851fa9e841ef2d593fbfff5a26c51c79231a0b2c2af5b41fb5a1ef2a"], ["HG22", () => "The weekly deeper practice\n\nOnce or twice a week, give the same practice more time when that is useful. More time needn\u2019t mean greater intensity. Start from Higher Self or neutral witnessing, follow what appears, borrow an adult function when needed, and ask whether anything important is being bypassed. Leave room for the full exit and any ordinary action needed afterward.\n\nDo the ordinary action immediately when possible. That teaches the subconscious that the adult heard and acted, instead of staging another beautiful internal meeting and then leaving the dishes exactly where they were.", "cde5fab9d639c83da878c1983d57303e9d0e0087f45a5b0b8cff25cecf78ad25"], ["HG23", () => "Capacity before content\n\nAltered-state rule: do not ask for hidden truth while medically unsafe, panicking, sleep-deprived, nauseated, destabilized, manic, psychosis-like, or chemically overwhelmed. First stabilize. Truth that requires destabilization is not ready to be known directly. Ask for capacity, not content.\n\nSometimes it feels as though the subconscious knows something the conscious mind can\u2019t yet tolerate. The subject may be sexuality, grief, trauma, family truth, betrayal, shame, vocation, relationship reality, illness fears, spiritual calling, addiction, identity, or moral conflict.\n\nInterrogation is the dangerous response:\n\n\u201cTell me now.\u201d \u201cReveal everything.\u201d \u201cStop hiding.\u201d \u201cI demand the truth.\u201d\n\nThat pressure can produce flooding, panic, false certainty, confabulation, dissociation, or a generally worse Tuesday. Flooding can make the adult capacities needed for pausing, comparing options, reality-testing, planning, and choosing much harder to reach. In that state, regulation and support come before deeper dialogue. Silence may be protection when the adult lacks enough regulation, support, humility, or practical capacity to receive the material.\n\nAsk for the next tolerable piece:\n\n\u201cDo I need to know the content now, or do I need to build capacity first?\u201d\n\n\u201cWhat would make the conscious mind 5% more able to receive this?\u201d\n\n\u201cCan you show me the truth as a symbol, not a full revelation?\u201d\n\n\u201cCan you give me the next protective action without revealing the whole story?\u201d\n\n\u201cWho, if anyone, should help me hold this?\u201d\n\nA safer script:\n\n\u201cWise subconscious, if there is something I cannot yet handle, please do not break it open violently. Help me become someone who can know it safely. Give me preparation before revelation. Give me grounding before memory. Give me support before intensity. Give me one next action.\u201d\n\nHypnotic imagery can be meaningful without being literal. The mind symbolizes, compresses, distorts, dramatizes, and borrows from culture and fear. Around trauma and memory especially, treat an image as an image and involve outside support when needed.", "e30364bf9aacf02af02640a6475ee87af9253887811a738aed4d2d904fe53ecf"], ["HG24", () => "Troubleshooting\n\nNothing happens\n\nStart smaller:\n\n\u201cShow me one body sensation.\u201d \u201cShow me one word.\u201d \u201cShow me whether today is for listening or resting.\u201d\n\nIt feels fake\n\nFirst ask what feels fake: the wording, the image, the expectation, or the role you\u2019re trying to perform. Not experiencing an inner voice or image isn\u2019t, by itself, resistance or a missing adult capacity. Correct an explicitly unwanted invitation. If you recognize a protective part, you can talk to it:\n\n\u201cWhat are you protecting me from?\u201d \u201cAre you afraid I will lie to myself again?\u201d \u201cAre you afraid this is performance?\u201d\n\nThe voice may be the inherited parent enforcing an old belonging rule. Don\u2019t argue with it. Ask which rule it is enforcing and what ordinary evidence would make the new adult voice more believable.\n\nThe fake feeling can also mean that a needed adult capacity isn\u2019t available yet. Stop demanding a convincing voice. If you can already observe what is happening, borrow the particular warmth, boundary, or direction you lack. A real or imagined caring figure can help. Try one believable sentence or action and see how it is received.\n\nThe subconscious will not answer\n\nThere may be no answer, or the question may not help. You can change it, rest, or finish. If a concern about trust is actually present, ask:\n\n\u201cWhat do you need to know before you trust me?\u201d\n\nThe reply may be extremely un-mystical: \u201cWill you set the boundary?\u201d \u201cWill you keep the promise?\u201d \u201cWill you stop abandoning us?\u201d \u201cWill you stop substituting insight for action?\u201d\n\nThe inner child will not appear\n\nIf a guard is recognizable, speak to it; otherwise stay with the feeling or preference that is actually present.\n\n\u201cYou do not have to let me in. Just tell me what would make the doorway safer.\u201d\n\nIf no clear child-state appears, use the session to notice one preference, curiosity, or beginner experiment instead of forcing an image. Identity-building and reparenting can happen in the same practice.\n\nYou get anxious\n\nNotice whether the anxiety is manageable and you want to stay with it, or whether you need less contact. When it is escalating or overwhelming, stop searching for content and help the body first.\n\nFeet. Chair. Breath. Room.\n\n\u201cLittle one, I am here. Protector, thank you for warning me. You do not have to scream for me to listen. If there is real action needed, show me one step. If there is no action needed, help the body stand down gently.\u201d\n\nGet professional help when anxiety includes chest pain, fainting, suicidal thoughts, psychosis-like or mania-like symptoms, severe dissociation, inability to sleep, or urges to harm yourself or someone else. Appendix D has the longer anxiety toolbox.\n\nYou dissociate or feel unreal\n\nOpen your eyes. Move. Touch something textured. Name five objects. End the deeper practice for the day.\n\nYou become grandiose\n\nReturn to food, sleep, chores, humility, and somebody who will disagree with you.\n\n\u201cIf this insight is real, it will survive sleep, food, time, humility, and feedback.\u201d\n\nYou become too analytical\n\nWhen the practice turns into a technical performance, stop improving it. Put one hand on the heart and one on the solar plexus.\n\n\u201cI am not here to do this perfectly. I am here to be in relationship with what is deeper than my surface mind.\u201d\n\n\u201cWhat would make this feel more like communion and less like performance?\u201d\n\nSilence may be the answer. Rest may be the answer. One honest action may be the answer. Take the answer that actually arrived.\n\nYou become too blissful or diffuse\n\nAsk the bliss to become specific.\n\n\u201cWhat task, boundary, meal, rest, repair, or conversation does love require next?\u201d\n\nYou want the hidden truth immediately\n\nDon\u2019t pry.\n\n\u201cShow me the capacity before the content.\u201d\n\nYou keep daydreaming\n\nSit up and open your eyes.\n\n\u201cWhat real action is this fantasy replacing?\u201d\n\nYou get stuck in spiritual bypass\n\n\u201cWhere has divine love not yet descended into the wound?\u201d\n\nA genuine opening can show you a depth of love or freedom you hadn\u2019t known was possible. Let that matter without demanding the child receive it on command. Prayer can also evoke abandonment or condemnation, or a teacher\u2019s betrayal can damage something sacred. Ask what was lost and what needs questioning; more of the same practice isn\u2019t automatically the answer.\n\nSometimes practical care helps while the available horizon of life still feels too small. Curiosity about deeper happiness is worth following without pretending to believe an explanation. Other people already have enough love and meaning for the life they want. Spirituality doesn\u2019t need to become another assignment.\n\nYou get stuck in endless processing\n\nA real wound and a checking loop can coexist. Ask whether this round helps you feel, understand, decide, or respond differently\u2014or whether you\u2019re checking the same question for reassurance. Grief and practice can revisit familiar ground; novelty isn\u2019t required every time.\n\nWhen it is a checking loop, let the thought remain without arguing with it or chasing it away, and return to a chosen activity or relationship. You don\u2019t have to hold an inner meeting every time a thought appears or complete a ritual before you\u2019re allowed to live.\n\n\u201cWhat direction would the Leader choose now?\u201d\n\nYou keep using hypnosis but not changing life\n\n\u201cWhat would prove to the inner child that the adult is real?\u201d\n\nThen do the thing.", "c1910795fed523f874f288955ae8f7caed7fa9a8b4158a2e66f63d8a2d7c5570"], ["HG25", () => "What not to do\n\nDo not treat inner voices, images, autonomous movements, or body signals as commands or proof. Listen, then reality-test.\n\nDo not chase grand downloads or turn demons, jinn, and \u201cUnattached Burdens\u201d into a hobby. Stabilize first, and seek help when reality-testing or function weakens.\n\nDo not use self-hypnosis while driving, bathing, swimming, operating equipment, supervising children, or doing anything that requires active attention.\n\nDo not force memory recovery, use trance to prove what happened, or suppress medical symptoms.\n\nDo not override hunger, fatigue, pain, grief, anger, or fear before understanding the signal.\n\nDo not prolong altered states indefinitely or treat intuition as fact without testing it.\n\nKeep the inner roles in proportion: the child does not lead adult life; the Protector cannot exclude all love; the Nurturer cannot erase consequences; the Leader cannot bypass the child; analysis and tenderness both remain available.\n\nDo not use NLP to override protectors, manufacture certainty, manipulate anyone, or perform healing when the body says no.\n\nDoubt, distraction, resistance, silence, and \u201cno\u201d do not prove that an induction is working.\n\n\u201cThe unconscious will keep me safe\u201d does not replace clear limits, a safe setting, the ability to stop, and a tested exit.\n\nOrdinary sensations do not prove hidden trauma, illness, toxicity, possession, attachment, transformation, or subconscious truth.\n\nImagined repair does not establish physical recovery, neurological repair, cellular change, or a cure.\n\nDo not install hidden instructions, undisclosed third-party activation, compulsory behavior, identity switching, or amnesia. A consciously chosen reusable cue should have a clear purpose, remain reviewable, and be retired if it becomes unwanted; saying \u201ccancel\u201d does not guarantee instant erasure.\n\nEnd every waking session with ordinary identity, movement, orientation, judgment, and a direct statement that the session has ended.", "74f31607f8026fad56501d82cef22b74f51f9d26444023191c03c00c39ebb6ea"], ["HG26", () => "When this needs more support than a guide can provide\n\nUse self-hypnosis as a relationship with yourself. When the situation needs actual care, the guide shouldn\u2019t become a substitute for it.\n\nGet urgent help now for immediate danger, severe confusion, inability to keep yourself safe, or concerning physical symptoms such as chest pain or fainting. New or worsening suicidal thoughts, severe insomnia, mania-like or psychosis-like symptoms, or dissociation that disrupts functioning need prompt professional assessment, not a four-to-twelve-week practice schedule.\n\nFor ordinary learning, start with shorter sessions, warmth, a manageable subject, and a full return. Over a week or two, track one target\u2014anxiety, daydreaming, relationship repair, an avoided task, sleep, emotional regulation, or intuitive accuracy. Worsening function or growing certainty about unverifiable memories calls for reassessment, not more intensity.\n\nSeek suitable ongoing help for recurring panic, compulsive behavior, substance-related destabilization, or major relationship instability.\n\nMethods to explore with a trained person present\n\nUse a trauma-informed clinician trained in the method, present with you, for deliberate trauma-focused regression, searching for unknown traumatic origins through an affect or somatic bridge, or work with substantial flashbacks or dissociative reactions. A bridge follows associations from a feeling or sensation; regression revisits recalled or imagined younger experience. Either can have different uses, including resourcing. Ask what is intended and how uncertainty and unexpected reactions will be handled. Hypnosis cannot prove a historical event, even with a clinician present.\n\nTrauma history, tears, or wanted difficult feelings don\u2019t automatically exclude self-practice. Choose the working range with attention to the actual response and available support. Some people need a narrow range; others much broader. Confidence alone doesn\u2019t supply missing support.\n\nMajor trauma guidelines emphasize treatments such as trauma-focused CBT variants, CPT, PE, and EMDR depending on the person and guideline. Self-hypnosis and inner-child practice may support that process, but high-risk trauma work needs a larger container.\n\nIf the practice makes ordinary life more loving, honest, functional, relational, grounded, and precise, continue gently. If it makes ordinary life less stable, stop deepening and return to sleep, food, grounding, boundaries, and outside support.", "05336eb189cf188a59f362da4f046f7152f828d1f0c5264693dd5884b5f03e28"], ["HG27", () => "Closing\n\nThe subconscious isn\u2019t the enemy. Neither are the body, the sharp analytical mind, or the protector that has been braced for years. Most of them have been trying to help without ever being properly introduced.\n\nI\u2019m less interested in how deeply you can get hypnotized than whether you become an adult you can trust.\n\nListen until the kid is willing to come closer. Keep enough small promises that the guards can occasionally sit down. Love yourself enough that thought stops bracing against tenderness, and think clearly enough that love doesn\u2019t drift into fantasy. Then take the practice back into ordinary life, where the adult keeps the promise.", "a97813e22c9e9b3b59efc145306bb3cfd01b01cdf78a502cf6a2240a312f2321"], ["HG28", () => "Try it in the Inner Signal app\n\nUse the Inner Signal app to get voice hypnosis sessions, learn the method, develop your own language, reflect on what you report, and ask questions about a hypnotist\u2019s approach. Self-hypnosis doesn\u2019t require an app, and finding a practitioner is an option rather than a graduation requirement.\n\nIn the ChatGPT version, an uploaded picture or drawing, a poem, or a song can be the starting point rather than a prose explanation. Use material the current chat can actually inspect; it should say when it cannot open an image or hear audio. What the material contains, what it means to you, and the app\u2019s interpretation are different things. You can correct the interpretation without having to justify your choice of expression.\n\nKeep in mind that even with voice, an app has to ask what is happening. It cannot feel your body, share a practitioner\u2019s embodied intuition, or know an unreported response. Its role is to help you learn and make better-informed choices, not pretend to be a skilled therapist.\n\nEnter through this page rather than bookmarking the app itself, so you reach the current version.\n\nPlease fill out the survey with what you learned. It gives you a record for later analysis and helps other people learn how the guide is landing in real practice.\n\nWhen the guide or app leaves a real question unanswered, you can contact me directly for a consultation.", "485ebb543deb4a2279aadff693267dad301c6109825bfc4bcc619c1166cdcdfd"], ["HG29+30", () => "Appendices\n\nThe main path is enough for daily practice. Everything below is optional: extra precision, extra safety, and specialized scripts for particular situations.\n\nAppendix A: How to ask the subconscious a useful question\n\nThe subconscious isn\u2019t an AI model, but prompt engineering offers one useful lesson: the shape of a question changes what kind of answer can appear. A prompt should make you more present, honest, loving, and able to hear the next signal\u2014never more controlling.\n\nI use seven elements when I want a prompt to be precise:\n\nRole: who or what you are addressing.\n\nContext: what situation you are bringing.\n\nIntention: what kind of change is desired.\n\nConstraint: safety, truth, kindness, no forcing, no false certainty, and capacity before content.\n\nOutput: image, phrase, body signal, memory, next action, question, symbol, silence, or rest.\n\nEvaluation: true, kind, timely, stabilizing, specific, testable.\n\nIteration: revise after life gives feedback.\n\nAlways include a safe fallback so the analytical mind doesn\u2019t treat silence as failure and manufacture an answer.\n\n\u201cAnd if the system needs more time to process this, or if silence is the safest answer today, let my body settle into deeper comfortable rest instead. A peaceful silence is a fully successful answer.\u201d\n\nSometimes nothing answers because nothing is happening. Sometimes the answer is not yet, rest first, build capacity, or do the ordinary thing you already know to do.\n\nCore prompts\n\nFor clarity:\n\n\u201cWise subconscious, please show me what is true, kind, and timely. If the full truth of this situation is too intense for my body to integrate today, pass it to my conscious mind only as a gentle symbol, a body signal, or a single next step. Show me my capacity to hold peace before you show me the content of the wound.\u201d\n\nFor resistance:\n\n\u201cWhat are you protecting me from, and what would help you trust me 1% more?\u201d\n\nFor the inner child:\n\n\u201cLittle one, what do you need me to understand, and what can I do today so you are not alone with it?\u201d\n\nFor the Protector:\n\n\u201cWhat real-world action would make this system safer?\u201d\n\nFor the Leader/Guru:\n\n\u201cWhat direction serves truth, love, life, and the benefit of all beings?\u201d\n\nFor intuition:\n\n\u201cLet intuition offer a signal, let analysis examine its meaning, let the body give feedback, and let reality test the interpretation.\u201d\n\nFor separating intuition from conditioning:\n\n\u201cWise subconscious, please help me look at this impulse clearly. If part of this signal belongs to family programming, religious fear, marketing, erotic imprinting, political tribe, trauma response, or a childhood bargain for belonging, let that part become visible as conditioning. Let it soften or step back only as much as is safe. Leave behind whatever belongs to true guidance, the universal heart, and a better ordinary life. Let my body feel the difference.\u201d\n\nFor daydreaming:\n\n\u201cIf this daydream is useful, reveal the guidance inside it. If it is avoidance, bring me gently into the present and help me deal with what I need to deal with.\u201d\n\nFor anxiety:\n\n\u201cWhat is anxiety trying to protect, and what would help it trust the adult self?\u201d\n\nFor hidden truth:\n\n\u201cIf the full truth would overwhelm me, please do not reveal it all. Show me the capacity before the content.\u201d\n\nFor integration:\n\n\u201cWhat ordinary-life action would prove that I heard correctly?\u201d\n\nThe prompt defines the search. NLP language affects how gently the question enters. Used together, they can create a safer field for an answer without pretending the answer is automatically true.", "5f602615c896455fbbce7fb60d9f6c0cbc9f7bfee2499c87afef9cb8906b631a"], ["HG31", () => "Appendix B: NLP without turning yourself into a sales seminar\n\nYou can practice the core method without learning one term of Neuro-Linguistic Programming. Read this appendix when you want more precision about imagery, bodily states, rehearsal, and sentence design. NLP is useful when it helps the system listen; it becomes a bag of tricks when technique replaces love, truth, contact, or consent.\n\nThe four NLP layers\n\nSensory NLP changes the form of an inner experience: image, sound, distance, size, brightness, volume, posture, pressure.\n\nState NLP makes useful states easier to access: warmth, adult presence, courage, steadiness, love, clarity.\n\nStrategy NLP rehearses a better response: when this cue appears, the adult does this.\n\nLanguage NLP shapes the sentence: pace what is already true, open awareness, presume only safe capacity, link cause and effect carefully, embed invitations, and use de-hypnotic questions when language becomes vague, absolute, manipulative, or grandiose.\n\nThe Milton Model, minus the covert sales seminar\n\nThe Milton Model concerns the architecture of the sentence. Commands such as \u201cRelax now,\u201d \u201cStop being anxious,\u201d or \u201cTrust me\u201d often make a part brace.\n\nPart of the point of indirect language is to get past that reflexive skeptical argument without having to overpower it. The destination can still be deliberate even when the route is subtle.\n\nBegin with what is already true, lower the pressure, presume only a small safe capacity, and invite one movement:\n\n\u201cAs the body notices the bed beneath it, it may become a little easier to stop holding the whole day alone.\u201d\n\nPace reality. Open awareness. Validate function. Presuppose capacity. Bridge gently. Invite one small movement. Test with the body.\n\nPattern 1: Pacing and leading \u2014 truth before suggestion\n\nPacing and leading means stating what is already true before inviting anything new.\n\n\u201cAnxiety is here. The body is activated. A protector is trying to help. And one breath at a time, the adult can start to listen.\u201d\n\nThe simplest form is a Truth + Invite loop:\n\n\u201cIt is completely true that [current sensation or emotion] is here. It makes sense that my system is reacting by [current protective behavior or thought]. And right alongside that truth, my adult self is here, breathing in this room, ready to listen to whatever comes up next.\u201d\n\nPattern 2: Awareness verbs\n\nAwareness verbs point attention without yelling at it: notice, realize, recognize, sense, feel, wonder, imagine, picture, listen, allow, discover, remember, consider.\n\n\u201cNotice whether the answer comes as an image, a word, a pressure, a warmth, a silence, or a next action.\u201d\n\nPattern 3: Presuppositions\n\nA presupposition hides an assumption inside a sentence. Use that power only to presume choice, timing, capacity, safety, love, and adult leadership. Don\u2019t smuggle in obedience.\n\n\u201cBefore any part has to change, it can first become more understood.\u201d\n\nPattern 4: Gentle cause-effect bridges\n\nWords such as as, while, because, when, after, before, and which means connect one event to another. Use them carefully. A gentle voice doesn\u2019t make a false causal claim true.\n\nToo strong: \u201cBecause I breathe, the trauma leaves.\u201d Better: \u201cAs I breathe, the adult self may become present enough to hear one small piece of what the wound needs.\u201d\n\nPattern 5: Embedded invitations\n\nAn embedded invitation places a small direction inside a larger sentence. It should invite a modest inner movement, not hide a demand.\n\n\u201cAnd somewhere inside this breath, I can let the adult self listen.\u201d\n\n\u201cAs I sit here, I may allow one small truth to become clear.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe child does not have to trust me all at once; it can simply notice I am still here.\u201d\n\nUse one when it helps. Ten embedded invitations in a row feel like somebody took a weekend certification.\n\nPattern 6: Ethical double binds\n\nA manipulative double bind traps every answer inside the speaker\u2019s agenda. An ethical one offers two genuinely safe routes.\n\n\u201cWould this part prefer warmth first, or space first?\u201d\n\nPattern 7: Conversational postulates\n\nThese are questions whose ordinary conversational form also acts as an invitation.\n\n\u201cCan the system show me the next step without showing me the whole wound?\u201d\n\nPattern 8: Tag questions\n\nTag questions\u2014isn\u2019t it, can\u2019t it, doesn\u2019t it\u2014can soften a sentence. They become oily very quickly, don\u2019t they? Use them lightly.\n\n\u201cThe body already knows something about resting, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\n\nPattern 9: Nominalizations\n\nNominalizations turn processes into nouns: healing, safety, trust, integration, communion, clarity, rest. Their vagueness can sometimes help because the subconscious supplies its own concrete meaning.\n\n\u201cA little more safety can arrive in whatever way the body understands.\u201d\n\nPattern 10: Meta Model questions \u2014 the de-hypnosis tool\n\nThe Milton Model opens possibility. The Meta Model asks exactly what somebody means. Ethical practice needs both.\n\n\u201cHow specifically?\u201d \u201cAccording to whom?\u201d \u201cCompared to what?\u201d \u201cAlways?\u201d \u201cNever?\u201d \u201cWhat exactly would happen?\u201d \u201cHow do I know?\u201d \u201cWhat evidence would change my mind?\u201d\n\nUse Milton Model to soften into the body. Use Meta Model to return to truth.\n\nSensory, state, and strategy tools\n\nSubmodalities \u2014 editing the form, not denying the truth\n\nFear, craving, fantasy, belief, and memory may have sensory qualities: near or far, bright or dim, loud or quiet, moving or still, heavy or light, inside or outside the body. Work with qualities you actually notice. If none are apparent, skip this tool rather than inventing an image or voice.\n\nAsk how the experience is represented. What changes when the image moves two feet back, the voice gets quieter, or the edge becomes less sharp?\n\n\u201cI am not deleting the signal. I am adjusting the volume so I can listen without being overwhelmed.\u201d\n\nBefore moving one imaginary pixel, ask the guard whether that change is acceptable.\n\n\u201cWould it be okay if we moved this image back just a little, or lowered the volume slightly, purely so my adult self can stay present enough to hear what you need to tell me?\u201d\n\nAnchoring \u2014 giving the body a doorway back\n\nAn anchor is a cue connected to a state: a hand on the heart, two fingers touching, a breath, a word, a posture, scent, or song. Find a real state you want easier access to, feel it, and pair the cue while it is clearly present with the phrase, \u201cThis is available.\u201d For hypnotic re-entry, choose a usable receptive state rather than peak emotional intensity; follow \u201cA shortcut back into hypnosis\u201d in the main induction section. Keep alert calming and the synchronicity wake-up cue separate.\n\nUsing synchronicity as your own wake-up bell\n\nPeople notice a synchronicity and immediately ask what it means. Sometimes that opens insight; sometimes it starts compulsive pattern-hunting. I prefer using the moment of noticing as a wake-up bell. The coincidence doesn\u2019t have to contain a message. It has already captured attention, which is enough.\n\nThe cue I give myself is:\n\n\u201cWhenever I notice something that feels like a synchronicity, I wake up inside the state I am in. I feel my body and the room. I remember that the coincidence is not proof of an interpretation. I let attention widen beyond the story my current mood is telling. I ask what is fact, what is interpretation, what needs warmth, what needs protection, and what needs action. I return to the highest perspective I can access without leaving the body, the facts, or ordinary human reality.\u201d\n\nI make room for whatever is genuinely available: objectivity, clarity, ease, peace, freedom, joy, and gratitude. I do not have to manufacture any of them.\n\nSynchronicity noticed \u2192 pause \u2192 orient \u2192 widen \u2192 reality-test \u2192 listen \u2192 choose one loving action.\n\nThe cue interrupts identification without erasing the state\u2019s message. A negative state may still contain an inner-child need, a Protector warning, grief, a bodily problem, or a real demand for action.\n\nInstalling the synchronicity cue\n\nImagine noticing an ordinary, harmless synchronicity.\n\nFeel the moment when attention says, \u201cWhoa.\u201d\n\nUse that opening to feel the feet, body, breath, and room.\n\nRepeat: \u201cThe noticing is my wake-up bell, not proof of a message.\u201d\n\nAsk the Nurturer, Protector, and Leader what each sees.\n\nRehearse returning to a grounded, wider perspective.\n\nEnd with one small action and the complete waking exit.\n\nThe cue remains self-authored, visible, reversible, and limited to orientation. It can\u2019t authorize a decision, prove an intuition, suppress a warning, or let another person control the response.\n\nPsychologically, this resembles an implementation intention: an advance \u201cif X, then Y\u201d plan that links a recognizable cue with a chosen response. Gollwitzer\u2019s foundational paper supports the general mechanism. This particular use of synchronicity hasn\u2019t been studied directly.\n\nThe same mechanism creates the risk: rehearsing synchronicity as a cue may make you scan for it. Pattern perception can drift toward false-positive meaning, especially during absorption or destabilization. Research links stronger apophenic pattern detection with false-positive judgments and psychosis-spectrum traits, although ordinary pattern noticing is not itself pathological. See Blain and colleagues and Grant and colleagues.\n\nDeactivate the cue if it increases compulsive scanning, sleeplessness, anxiety, certainty that random events are personally directed messages, or difficulty distinguishing observation from interpretation.\n\nTo retire it, stop rehearsing it and say while fully awake: \u201cSynchronicity is no longer a cue. I release this association and return coincidence to ordinary uncertainty.\u201d Remove it from the written cue list and stop rehearsing it. The statement expresses your decision; it does not prove the association has vanished immediately.\n\nReframing \u2014 changing the meaning without lying\n\nDishonest reframing declares that the problem was secretly good all along. I prefer questions that widen meaning without lying:\n\n\u201cWhat else might be true?\u201d \u201cWhat is this part trying to do for me?\u201d \u201cWhat would the Nurturer, the Protector, and the Leader each say about this?\u201d\n\nPerceptual positions \u2014 self, other, witness, universal heart\n\nFirst: what do I feel from inside myself? Second: if I sit inside the other person, what might they be feeling or protecting? Third: if I watched this from across the room, what would I notice? Fourth: from the universal heart, what serves truth, love, boundaries, and the good of everyone involved?\n\nDon\u2019t use perceptual positions to excuse harm. Use them to escape the prison of one angle.\n\nFuture pacing \u2014 let the future test the insight\n\nDon\u2019t end with \u201cI understand.\u201d Ask where life will test the understanding, picture the actual cue, and rehearse the smallest better response.\n\n\u201cIf [cue], then I [small action].\u201d\n\nThe Swish, softened\n\nClassic NLP uses the Swish to replace an old self-image with a new one. Done mechanically, it can feel like an inner eviction. Here, the old pattern stays visible, the new possibility stands beside it, and I notice the body\u2019s response while comparing what each future would actually require.\n\nParts integration, without pretending the conflict is solved\n\n\u201cWhat does one side want?\u201d \u201cWhat does the other side fear?\u201d \u201cWhat need sits under both?\u201d \u201cWhat third option honors both without letting either run the whole life?\u201d\n\nWhat I will not use NLP for\n\nTo talk myself out of a real signal.\n\nTo override fear before asking what the fear protects.\n\nTo recover memories.\n\nTo manipulate anyone else.\n\nTo convince myself an intuition is true.\n\nTo erase pain, hunger, grief, anger, or a medical symptom.\n\nWhile panicking, destabilized, manic, psychosis-like, chemically overwhelmed, or too dissociated to reality-test.\n\nTo make another person\u2019s no feel like resistance.\n\nTo make someone feel spiritually inferior for disagreeing.\n\nTo hide a command inside soft language.\n\nTo create urgency where time is needed.\n\nTo bypass consent by calling something \u201csubconscious agreement.\u201d\n\nTo use trance, fatigue, attraction, shame, grief, or altered states to get compliance.\n\nRunning the whole NLP sequence\n\nInduce or contain. Gather attention, or stabilize first if you are already wide open.\n\nAsk permission. Is it useful to work with this image, feeling, or pattern today?\n\nRepresent the pattern. How does this show up \u2014 image, voice, sensation, posture, distance, size, color, movement?\n\nAsk what it protects. What is this trying to do for me?\n\nAdjust the form, gently. Move it back, lower the volume, add space, change posture, slow the scene.\n\nBring in the three adults. Nurturer warms, Protector makes it safe, Leader points a direction.\n\nAnchor the useful state. Heart, fingers, breath, posture.\n\nFuture pace. If the old cue shows up, what is the smallest new action?\n\nCheck the fit and the evidence. What changes in the body? Is the response kind and doable? What supports the interpretation beyond how it feels?\n\nExit and act. Come back to the room. Do one ordinary thing.", "b558346b2487bc6723d043d85d7d23b2626d6b4352a60f4fba7d0de6ce63e827"], ["HG32", () => "Appendix C: The extended de-hypnosis checklist\n\nUse this checklist when a person, advertisement, ideology, spiritual teaching, romantic pull, or inner voice is pressing too hard.\n\n\u201cI notice influence is happening. I do not need to obey it or attack it. I can slow down. I can ask what is being presupposed. I can ask who benefits. I can notice what my body is signaling without assuming what it proves. I can ask what love, truth, humility, and ordinary life would require.\n\nIf this is true, it will survive questions. If this is love, it will survive boundaries. If this is guidance, it will survive sleep. If this is for me, it will not require me to abandon my adult self.\u201d\n\nBefore acting, ask:\n\nIs there urgency?\n\nIs there secrecy?\n\nIs there shame?\n\nIs there a threat of exclusion?\n\nIs there a promise of specialness?\n\nIs there sexual charge?\n\nIs there spiritual superiority?\n\nIs there a demand for money, sex, loyalty, silence, or obedience?\n\nIs there a narrowing of choices?\n\nIs questioning treated as betrayal?\n\nIs resistance pathologized?\n\nIs ordinary life being downgraded?\n\nIs the child part being seduced, scared, or rescued?\n\nIs the adult self still allowed to say no?\n\nSeveral yes answers mean slow down. Don\u2019t deepen trance or make an irreversible decision. Return to the room, body, food, sleep, time, and counsel.\n\nA good guide increases your adult self. A bad guide replaces it. A good guide can tolerate your no. A bad guide calls your no resistance, ego, fear, low vibration, trauma, sin, or proof that you need more of their guidance.\n\nHypnosis-specific audit\n\nBefore accepting a recording or live suggestion, ask:\n\nWas I plainly told that induction mechanics are being used?\n\nCan at least one answer lead to pause, modification, full alertness, or stopping?\n\nDoes the speaker treat not noticing as secret proof that the process worked?\n\nAre roles, ownership, obedience, age, identity, or belonging being used to lower adult judgment?\n\nAre fractionation, cues, later behavior, or memory effects disclosed before they are used?\n\nAre ordinary body sensations being turned into evidence for a hidden claim?\n\nDoes the exit restore ordinary identity, movement, orientation, and critical judgment\u2014and plainly say that the session has ended?\n\nWhen an answer is unclear, stay fully alert. Ask for the exact script, change the method, or leave.", "261525de5db38b82a4b42db37a4a03c62203d131cbcb80220084e1d1cb1c16b3"], ["HG33", () => "Appendix D: Anxiety \u2014 body first\n\nAnxiety is often a Protector trying to prevent danger, shame, abandonment, failure, exposure, illness, rejection, loss of control, or a feeling it believes would be unbearable. Don\u2019t begin by cross-examining it. Help the body first.\n\nBody first. Inner child second. Inquiry third. Action fourth.\n\nInner child love\n\nStart with the hand-on-heart, hand-on-solar-plexus practice.\n\n\u201cLittle one, you are not alone. You do not have to solve this. I am here now. The adult is listening.\u201d\n\nDon\u2019t recite the words like an incantation you\u2019re hoping the subconscious will grade. Say them the way you\u2019d speak to a frightened child you actually love.\n\nReduced breathing / Buteyko-style breathing\n\nUse this gently. Buteyko-style reduced breathing can help some people who overbreathe or panic, but don\u2019t force breath holds. Air hunger can briefly increase anxiety before it settles, so go slowly.\n\nBreathe through the nose. Let the inhale be small. Let the exhale be quiet. Pause only if comfortable. Let the breath become less dramatic. Stop if dizzy, strained, panicky, or medically concerned.\n\nEFT tapping\n\nEFT can be a simple bodily ritual: name the fear, tap gently, and stay in contact with what you feel. The evidence is still developing. A 2025 systematic review found seven randomized controlled trials involving EFT for anxiety disorders; EFT beat no intervention in several of them, although the evidence should still be treated cautiously beside first-line therapies.\n\n\u201cEven though this anxiety is here, I am willing to be kind to the part of me that is afraid.\u201d\n\nSomatic Experiencing\n\nMore info, and a free intro session on my Somatic Guide. With SE, we track gently. Don\u2019t turn the exercise into trauma excavation.\n\n\u201cWhere is the activation?\u201d \u201cWhere is the neutral place?\u201d \u201cCan attention move slowly between them?\u201d\n\nGendlin Focusing\n\n\u201cWhat is the felt sense of this whole anxiety?\u201d\n\nDon\u2019t answer from the head immediately. Give the body time to form one vague sense of the whole situation, then ask:\n\n\u201cWhat would this felt sense like me to know?\u201d\n\nTry a word or phrase and check how well it fits the felt sense. Then examine what it suggests about the situation; feeling right doesn\u2019t settle that. Words and bodily attention can help correct each other.\n\nBody supports\n\nSometimes the mind is too activated to meditate usefully. Then help through the body: foot, scalp, temple, neck, or shoulder massage; warmth; pressure; a weighted blanket; darkness; familiar calming music; or a massage gun used carefully. Avoid the front of the neck, injuries, numb areas, bruises, inflamed tissue, recent surgery sites, varicose veins, and anything that produces pain, dizziness, tingling, or a symptom that feels wrong.\n\nOther people settle with warmth, darkness, music, massage, weighted blankets, breathwork, familiar objects, or personal relaxation technology. Use the support that actually helps rather than assembling a tiny wellness trade show around the anxiety.\n\nPersonal relaxation-tech note: On my ZHoly3O page, I discuss ozonated charcoal \u201choly water\u201d as one relaxation support, and I also mention Blushield. These remain optional supports. They don\u2019t prove that any inner signal is true. Stabilize the body, listen gently, reality-test, and return to one grounded action.\n\nAnxiety script\n\nFirst, body. Feet, chair, breath, room.\n\nLittle one, I am here. Protector, thank you for warning me. You do not have to scream for me to listen.\n\nIf there is real action needed, show me one step. If there is no action needed, help the body stand down gently.\n\nI will not abandon the signal. I will not worship the signal. I will listen, test, and act.", "c88f4858487b2b65e59d78649ec25b10e4ae7913b1dd4016261a43855af8f843"], ["HG34", () => "Appendix E: Is the daydream helping or replacing your life?\n\nDaydreaming can be intuition, simulation, grief digestion, creative rehearsal, symbolic teaching, or the subconscious showing a possible future, fear, or desire. It can also become avoidance, fantasy addiction, dissociation, romantic obsession, or an escape hatch from adult life.\n\nDoes the daydream return me to life with more clarity, love, and action? Or does it pull me away from life into repetition, craving, and avoidance?\n\nWhen it seems useful, ask:\n\n\u201cWhat is the guidance inside this?\u201d\n\nWhen it seems to be pulling you away, change the body before debating the story. Sit upright. Open the eyes a little. Feel the feet.\n\n\u201cSubconscious, thank you for showing me images. If this daydream is useful, please reveal the guidance inside it. If it is avoidance, please bring me gently into the present and help me deal with what I need to deal with in a gentle but structured way.\u201d\n\nThen get embarrassingly practical:\n\n\u201cWhat real-life action is this daydream replacing?\u201d\n\nThe answer may be: send the message, stop sending the message, clean the room, eat, sleep, grieve, create, apologize, leave, tell the truth, make the plan, ask for help, return to the task, or stop chasing unavailable love.", "1d6d18c70d667206695575e8b650480c7fa44734ec82870fecf8f696979846dd"], ["HG35", () => "Appendix F: Self-trust, outside guidance, and oracle-like states\n\nThe synchronicity cue above turns noticing into orientation and reality-testing rather than an oracle.\n\nSuggestibility varies wildly. Some people open more with an external hypnotist, some with themselves, some only in particular states, and some barely respond to commands while responding deeply to imagery, music, metaphor, or spiritual feeling. A professional can bring pacing, containment, and another set of eyes. Self-hypnosis gives you instant follow-up, real-time correction, and a private line to your own protectors.\n\nSelf-hypnosis may be best for daily intimacy with the subconscious. Skilled outside support may be best for trauma complexity, blind spots, dissociation, destabilization, and relational wounds that need another nervous system.\n\nFinding a hypnotist who can work with you\n\nAsk what they are trained to treat, how they use hypnosis, and how they respond when a suggestion doesn\u2019t fit. For trauma work, ask about actual trauma and dissociation training, not only a hypnosis certificate. Credentials matter without guaranteeing competent care.\n\nDescribe what has helped, what hasn\u2019t, your preferred directness, acceptable touch and imagery, and how you\u2019ll stop. Ask about unexpected reactions, uncertain memories, and the full return. Firm encouragement can fit; refusal still ends it.\n\nEvaluate what actually happened, not the method\u2019s name. Was its purpose explained? Did you agree? How did the practitioner respond to you? Discomfort alone doesn\u2019t settle the question; treating every objection as proof you need more of the same deserves scrutiny. Ask the app or another informed person for explanation, without expecting a practitioner to be certified from a brief account. Self-hypnosis, combined work, or another practitioner remain your choices.\n\nWhen the subconscious speaks like an oracle\n\nThe subconscious can notice things the conscious mind misses: anticipations, pattern reads, and sometimes something uncanny. Respect the signal without turning a vivid image or directive into an order.\n\nWhen the message has large or irreversible consequences, slow the action to the scale of those consequences, ask the adult questions, and bring the message to a council.\n\nThe council \u2014 internal first, then outward\n\nThe first council is internal: child and adult, conscious and subconscious, intuition and analysis. Reach outside when the message is bigger than those parts can hold alone. The larger and less reversible the move, the more seriously you seek other minds.\n\nAsk people who love you, like-minded people, or somebody who has walked the path. Going alone can be legitimate, but I\u2019d make it the last step after counsel was genuinely sought.\n\nThe opposite danger is giving away your power. \u201cSeek counsel\u201d can quietly become \u201cfind an authority and obey.\u201d Another person deserves neither automatic trust nor automatic dismissal.\n\nPeer support can lend a different kind of help. In Hearthwork, equal time, warmth, confidentiality, and respect for \u201cno\u201d let each person lend Nurturer and Protector without taking over the other\u2019s Guide. Then you switch roles. A carefully timed question can help your own direction become audible; the peer doesn\u2019t decide what the experience means.\n\nForgiveness without bypass\n\nForgiveness can\u2019t be forced. It grows from understanding, and pushing it too early creates bypass. Staying angry forever can also let the injury keep governing the present. All of these can remain true together: the harm happened, the boundary still matters, the person remains accountable, and forgiveness may come later when your relationship to the event changes on its own.\n\nThe understanding that helps me is seeing how conditioning, history, pain, and capacity shaped the harmful move. Given the total person they had become by that moment, the harmful move was the only one their accumulated nature had available. The harm remains real, and so do accountability, consequences, and the work of making a different response possible now. Understanding someone\u2019s limitations should make expectations more realistic, not make the limitations less consequential.\n\n\u201cYou do not have to forgive before you are ready. I will not use spirituality to silence your pain. I will also not build our whole life around this wound forever.\u201d\n\nQuestions for forgiveness without erasing anything:\n\n\u201cWhat happened?\u201d \u201cWhat was the impact?\u201d \u201cWhat boundaries are still needed?\u201d \u201cWhat shaped this person into someone capable of that harm?\u201d \u201cWhat belongs to them?\u201d \u201cWhat belongs to me?\u201d \u201cWhat does accountability require?\u201d \u201cWhat would release look like without denial?\u201d\n\nFor self-forgiveness, name what you did without taking responsibility for what wasn\u2019t yours. Let remorse register the harm or violation of your values without making the person who acted a permanent criminal. Then move through responsibility, remorse, rectify, release: name the action and impact; allow the remorse; repair or prevent repetition where possible; and stop holding yourself hostage after responsibility has been faced.", "dfc07549a89cead9902c303356d3f0189d8c42520b421d1d6947bf87481eb240"], ["HG36", () => "Appendix G: Altered states\n\nThis guide doesn\u2019t tell anyone to use substances. It does acknowledge that many people first encounter self-hypnosis, inner-child contact, or Higher Self dialogue while altered.\n\nDo not use altered states to force revelation. Use them to deepen listening, strengthen love, and improve ordinary life.\n\nA past opening can be a resource without becoming a state you must reproduce. Having experienced love once, being able to access it only in a particular state, and contacting it reliably in daily life are different things. Let the experience widen what seems possible while you build usable access. If the child cannot receive it, meet the difficulty rather than escalating the intensity.\n\nDuring or after an altered state, these prompts are usually more useful than demanding a revelation:\n\n\u201cHelp me stay grounded.\u201d \u201cHelp me receive only what I can integrate.\u201d \u201cHelp me remember love.\u201d \u201cHelp me return when it is time.\u201d \u201cHelp me translate this into one ordinary-life change.\u201d\n\nDon\u2019t use self-hypnosis to prolong an altered state indefinitely, recover memories, intensify fear, bypass sleep, override medical needs, or make yourself less able to communicate.\n\nIf the state makes you less loving, less grounded, less honest, less able to sleep, less able to communicate, or less able to meet ordinary responsibilities, stop deepening and start integrating.\n\nStabilize before you interpret\n\nSelf-hypnosis can become very powerful during loveyhuasca, meditation, lucid dreaming, grief, sexual energy, prayer, sleep loss, or other plant-medicine-adjacent states. Sometimes the mind opens faster than the adult self can organize what is arriving.\n\nIntensity then gets mistaken for meaning. Sometimes the inner child is finally speaking. Sometimes a Protector is showing fear, or shame, grief, anger, longing, and other buried material are asking for care. But an intense state can also be bodily overload, environmental chaos, sleep deprivation, or plain confusion. A frightening image isn\u2019t automatically truth, a bad feeling isn\u2019t automatically a lesson, and a \u201cdownload\u201d doesn\u2019t deserve belief merely because it came with excellent lighting.\n\nBefore trying to interpret the state, ask a simpler question:\n\n\u201cWhat kind of problem is happening?\u201d", "cad956e71b2cbfca4cfc6dfec1a22370415ac29610a5ed77e66bcca048e31a16"], ["HG37", () => "Appendix H: Demons, jinn, hostile presences, and \u201cUnattached Burdens\u201d\n\nI\u2019m speaking from experience in this appendix.\n\nDuring self-hypnosis, meditation, lucid dreaming, loveyhuasca, grief, prayer, plant-medicine-adjacent practice, or other altered states, people sometimes meet something that doesn\u2019t feel like an ordinary emotion or younger part. It may appear as a demon, jinn, parasitic energy, dark intelligence, or terrifying archetype\u2014a voice or image that feels intrusive, hateful, seductive, mocking, or simply alien.\n\nI don\u2019t need this guide to settle the metaphysics. Perhaps these are literal beings. Perhaps they are symbolic mind-forms, trauma in a frightening costume, dissociated energy, or ancestral and relational burdens. The practical question is:\n\n\u201cWhat response makes me safer, clearer, more loving, more grounded, and less hooked?\u201d\n\nStart with the room, the body, and basic safety\n\nWhen a demonic, jinn-like, hostile, or intrusive presence appears, don\u2019t reward it with instant metaphysical certainty. Run the triage first: medical danger, environment, body, overload, and how open the mind has become. Open the eyes, feel the feet, name the room, lower stimulation, leave hostile company, and stop adding prompts. Don\u2019t deepen, hunt for hidden truth, or debate ontology while destabilized.\n\n\u201cOnly what can be integrated is welcome now. Anything too much can wait. The adult self remains present. The body may return to safety.\u201d\n\nDanger to self or others, psychosis-like fear, inability to sleep, extreme paranoia, chest pain, seizures, serious overheating, overdose concern, or loss of basic functioning means the self-hypnosis session is over. Get real-world help.\n\nA response check, not a verdict about what it is\n\nOnce you\u2019re stable enough to stay present, notice what helps without turning the response into a metaphysical test. Put a hand on the heart if that feels welcome, take a comfortable breath, and keep the room available.\n\nI don\u2019t have to decide what this is right now. I can notice whether warmth or distance helps, and keep my boundary either way. Nothing here requires my obedience.\n\nIf vulnerability appears\u2014a knot in the throat, sadness, fear, or a sense of trying to protect something\u2014you can bring in the Nurturer. If the experience remains hostile or becomes more frightening, stop engaging with it and return to the body and room.\n\nNeither response establishes its origin. A protective part needn\u2019t reveal its fear quickly; a comforting response doesn\u2019t prove an interpretation either. The useful question is whether this way of responding helps you become clearer, steadier, and less hooked. Love and boundaries don\u2019t require a verdict first.\n\nDo not fight from fear\n\nThe first impulse may be to fight, banish, hate, prove power, argue, panic, or identify completely with the threatened self. Some traditions use prayers, mantras, protections, banishing rituals, or exorcism forms, and some people find them useful. But fighting from fear can fasten attention more tightly to the thing you want gone.\n\nFear gives it center stage. Hatred gives it a hook. Pride gives it an opponent. Panic gives it the nervous system.\n\n\u201cI do not need to hate this. I do not need to merge with this. I do not need to obey this. I do not need to defeat this from fear. I return to loving awareness.\u201d\n\nA bodily boundary: compassion present, door closed\n\nWhen the presence feels hostile, cold, alien, parasitic, or boundary-violating, don\u2019t offer soft sentimental love as though inviting it in for tea. Use love with a boundary.\n\nSit upright. Press your feet into the floor. Let the spine rise. Gently draw the shoulder blades back so the chest is open but not exposed. Feel the skin boundary of the torso. Feel that this body has a perimeter.\n\n\u201cI recognize that suffering may be here, and I wish all suffering to return to the space where it belongs. But the boundary of this skin belongs to my adult life. You do not have permission to enter this house.\u201d\n\n\u201cCompassion is present. The door is closed.\u201d\n\nIf it might be psychosis or destabilization\n\nA symbolic hostile-presence experience that settles with grounding and love is very different from a state that becomes persistent, paranoid, sleepless, dangerous, or impossible to reality-test.\n\nIf somebody believes they\u2019re under attack for days, can\u2019t sleep or function, hears commands, feels compelled to hurt themselves or anyone else, becomes manic, can\u2019t communicate normally, or can\u2019t distinguish symbolic experience from ordinary reality, stop treating the problem as hypnosis material. Get help.\n\nIf the practice makes you more loving, grounded, relational, honest, and functional, continue gently. If it makes you more paranoid, sleepless, grandiose, isolated, frightened, or unreal, stop deepening and get support.\n\nThe cosmic-download humility test\n\nHostile presences, shadow forces, grandiose parts, and altered-state imagery don\u2019t always attack through fear. Sometimes they seduce through importance: a cosmic secret, a special mission, a vast download, a feeling of being uniquely chosen, or a revelation that makes washing dishes seem beneath your new rank.\n\n\u201cThank you for the imagery. I do not require a grand performance. If this information cannot become a simple, honest act of care tomorrow morning \u2014 washing the dishes, cleaning my room, keeping a promise, apologizing, resting, eating, or helping someone I love \u2014 it is not useful to my path.\u201d\n\nGuidance worth keeping should make ordinary life more beautiful, honest, relational, humble, reliable, and useful to other people.\n\nDon\u2019t become an entity hobbyist\n\nDemons, jinn, hostile presences, and \u201cUnattached Burdens\u201d all lead to the same practical training: can love remain present without surrendering the boundary, and can the mind become harder to hook? I\u2019m not trying to turn anyone into an entity hobbyist.", "ee9e4413efafe41641f4e0c60d172439635481c173bd7d758a47dd5bdf40712d"], ["HG38+39", () => "Appendix I: Sleep hypnosis\n\nFor sleep hypnosis, use Nimja\u2019s sleep playlist.\n\nAppendix J: A practical induction library\n\nPreview the sequence, choose one purpose, and decide whether you want to begin. Each family can be light, eyes-open, or replaced by no-trance contact. Notice the actual response\u2014including no change\u2014and let it determine the next move. Move, imagine, change the sensory channel, remain still, or stop. Use the complete waking return after every induction.\n\nIf vivid images don\u2019t come, translate the metaphor into words, rhythm, spatial sense, touch, movement, or simple knowing. The mind doesn\u2019t owe anybody a movie.\n\nStretch and release\n\nFeel the support beneath you and the position of one hand or foot. Press gently, stretch slightly, and release\u2014or imagine it. Notice ease, tension, warmth, breath, or no change. Repeat with no more than three areas, letting \u201crelease\u201d guide the sequence. Which area or inner voice wants attention? What support is needed? Restore ordinary movement and return.\n\nSupported floating\n\nNotice where your weight is carried. Let support carry 1% more effort, as if floating without losing contact, or keep ordinary weight and pressure. Notice the response. You may deliberately alternate attention toward ordinary awareness on the inhale and support on the exhale; name this fractionation beforehand. What becomes easier to hear? Keep the support, regain chosen weight and firm contact, then return.\n\nRhythmic breath or pulse\n\nAccompany a natural rhythm: breath, a comfortable hum, a finger tap, outside sound, or silent counting. No rhythm is also an option. Repeat \u201chere, and here again,\u201d noticing where attention lands between repetitions. Stop the deliberate rhythm and notice the first quiet beat, without trying to become blank. Is a word, feeling, need, or action clearer? Let ordinary rhythm resume and return.\n\nUnwinding object\n\nNotice a concern with several strands. Imagine yarn unwinding, bubbles settling, or knots placed visibly beside you. Leave the object unchanged or label it in words when that fits better. Count only strands you actually set down; nothing is erased. Which needs warmth, protection, or action? Keep one useful strand, give the others a visible holding place, and return.\n\nSensory journey\n\nHear the actual room and feel the body before imagining a step toward a chosen destination. Use footsteps, textures, or landmarks to gather attention. Change scenery or skip directly to dialogue when useful. Notice a detail without assigning hidden meaning; the mystery is how the scene develops, not what will be done to you. Who or what needs to speak? Return by the same route and close the imagined space.\n\nGradual wave\n\nStart with a small neutral area and what is already there. Imagine warmth, steadiness, or attention spreading to the next comfortable area, or keep it localized. Pause at each step and notice what changes without claiming medical repair. Does the body want contact, space, words, or ordinary care? Let the image recede, feel the whole body and real contact points, and return.\n\nCare workshop\n\nPrepare time, a place, tools, and boundaries for patient care. Let a signal, feeling, or younger state approach only as closely as welcome; choose language that doesn\u2019t make you feel fragmented. Ask before responding, wait, reconnect only where welcome, rest, and reassess. An available inner adult or one borrowed function can help; you choose words, pace, and action. Aim for \u201cgood as you,\u201d not erased history. Close the workspace, affirm wholeness and adult presence, and return.\n\nResource embodiment\n\nChoose qualities you understand: warmth, boundary, steadiness, curiosity, or dignity. Imagine posture, voice, or attention with 1% more access to one quality. Observe rather than embody it when preferred. Notice a movement, word, image, or no response. Repeat to strengthen access, not replace identity. How would that quality meet today\u2019s situation? Keep the quality and one useful action, release the role, affirm \u201cI am myself,\u201d and return.\n\nNo-trance inner contact\n\nObservable pacing: Keep the eyes open, name the room and day, feel the feet, and read or write in ordinary awareness. Invitation: Ask one clear question and wait without narrowing attention further. Genuine choice: Answer, write \u201cunsure,\u201d rest, change the question, seek another person\u2019s view, or stop. Autonomous signal: Record a word, sensation, impulse, objection, or silence without interpreting it as proof. Deepener: None. Full alertness is not an inferior version of the practice. Handoff: Let the internal adult respond in writing or through one practical choice. Integration and exit: Reality-test the signal and do one small action. Because no altered state was cultivated, simply close the exercise and continue the day.", "52b50cb5adbe713b08eb539a721a868f57594d4b724a05fd68934488f8466e97"], ["HG40", () => "Appendix K: Sources and further reading\n\nThe personal accounts and the way I arrange this practice are mine. The revised teaching brings them together with body-oriented and hypnotic approaches described below. That combination is an adaptation, not a treatment validated as a whole. These notes are for readers who want to follow an idea further; you don\u2019t need them open while practicing.\n\nLearning, entering, and returning\n\nMaggie Phillips and Claire Frederick, Healing the Divided Self (1995), especially pp.24\u201327, 45\u201350, and 83\u201390, discusses interactive hypnosis, sensory learning, self-hypnosis, and a definite return. It is a professional clinical book, not a self-help manual; its case procedures and historical claims shouldn\u2019t be imported wholesale into solo practice. Adam Eason and Benjamin Parris\u2019s ISH article on self-hypnosis discusses self-directed learning and its relationship to another hypnotist and recordings.\n\nIndirect language and re-entry cues\n\nPhillips and Frederick, pp.53\u201357 and 96\u2013100, explain pacing, utilization, metaphor, and associational cues. Nimja\u2019s hypnotic-language tutorial and trigger tutorial describe language and cueing techniques. They are technique sources, not evidence that every NLP claim is established or every example suits trauma work. The original wording and deliberately chosen re-entry sequence here are adaptations; Nimja did not write or endorse this guide.\n\nBody awareness and changing the amount of contact\n\nPeter Levine, In an Unspoken Voice (2010), chapters 5 and 7; Levine and Maggie Phillips, Freedom from Pain (2012), chapters 3\u20134; and Levine, Healing Trauma, chapter 4, inform bodily tracking, resources, titration, pendulation, and attention across sensation, imagery, behavior, emotion, and meaning. The invitation to try a movement or image is not a claim that a particular physiological repair has occurred.\n\nInner-child care and adult capacities\n\nThe companion Inner Child Self-Love Reparenting Guide gives the fuller treatment of borrowed adulthood, love without compulsory trust, practical and relational repair, spiritual struggle, differentiation, and mutual peer support. The corresponding practices here follow that account while retaining the self-hypnosis entry, responsive language, and complete return.\n\nHealing the Divided Self, pp.148\u2013153 and 171\u2013174, discusses renurturing, somatic work, and developing nurturing capacities. Levine and Maggie Kline\u2019s Trauma Through a Child\u2019s Eyes, especially chapters 4\u20135 and 10, discusses attunement and individual responses in actual children and caregiving. Applying those ideas to an adult\u2019s younger feeling-states is an adaptation. My three-adult arrangement and borrowed-adulthood sequence are not presented as those authors\u2019 tested protocol.\n\nInner speech and putting experience into words\n\nNedergaard and Lupyan\u2019s Not Everybody Has an Inner Voice (2024) studied people reporting different amounts of inner speech, not self-hypnosis adaptations. The questions in Quick start are a practical self-check, not a validated screening instrument or a test of hypnotic ability.\n\nMemory, support, and choosing a practitioner\n\nLevine\u2019s Trauma and Memory (2015), chapter 7, is useful on the difference between compelling experience and historical verification. The VA\u2019s hypnosis overview includes cautions about strong reactions and false memories. ASCH\u2019s explanation of certification distinguishes professional preparation from a guarantee of someone\u2019s competence. The existing links in the evidence and specialist sections point to their particular claims rather than validating every use of hypnosis.", "edaf23387730af13704b555e7c7f756345b29d4b5cf9dde2fb8044b2624bcd5f"]];
const rev = !!window.__reverse;
const items = rev ? items0.slice().reverse() : items0;
const order0 = items0.map(x => x[0]);
const lane = k => "B429" + '_' + k;
try { localStorage.removeItem(lane(rev ? 'rev' : 'fwd')); } catch (e) {}
window.__B429 = [];
const log = m => { window.__B429.push(m); try { localStorage.setItem(lane(rev ? 'rev' : 'fwd'), JSON.stringify(window.__B429)); } catch (e) {} };
const other = () => { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(lane(rev ? 'fwd' : 'rev')) || '[]').filter(m => / (clicked|taking)$/.test(m)).map(m => order0.indexOf(m.split(':')[0])); } catch (e) { return []; } };
const setter = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(HTMLTextAreaElement.prototype, 'value').set;
const sha = async t => Array.from(new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(t)))).map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
const button = () => [...document.querySelectorAll('button[type=submit]')].find(b => /check for ai/i.test(b.innerText));
const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
async function run() {
  for (const [k, mk, want] of items) {
    const o = other(), i = order0.indexOf(k);
    if (o.length && (rev ? i <= Math.max(...o) : i >= Math.min(...o))) { log('stop at ' + k + ': the other tab has the rest'); break; }
    log(k + ': taking');  // claimed before the work, so the other tab never takes it too (turn 65: with a margin
                          // of one instead, both tabs stopped short of the last check)
    const T = mk();  // built when its turn comes, so it can use texts stored earlier in this batch
    for (let j = 0; j < 20 && !(button() && document.querySelector('textarea')); j++) await wait(500);
    const ta = document.querySelector('textarea');
    if (!ta) { log(k + ': no textarea'); break; }
    setter.call(ta, T); ta.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', {bubbles: true}));
    let b = null;
    for (let j = 0; j < 30; j++) { await wait(500); b = button(); if (b && !b.disabled) break; }
    const h = await sha(ta.value);
    if (h !== want) { log(k + ': hash mismatch ' + h + ' (is its prefix in sessionStorage?)'); break; }
    if (!b || b.disabled) { log(k + ': button not ready'); break; }
    try { sessionStorage.setItem(k, T); } catch (e) {}
    b.click(); log(k + ': clicked');
    await wait(10000);
  }
  log('end');
}
run(); 'started'
